window.CHAPTERS = [{"id":1,"title":"Introduction","subtitle":"The boundaries of computation","group":"Foundations","slides":24,"sections":[{"page":2,"title":"“What are the","details":["fundamental capabilities and limitations of computers?”"]},{"page":3,"title":"Lecture detail · 3","details":["This fundamental question goes back to the 1930s, decades before modern electronic computers were built.","It began when researchers first attempted to mathematically define the exact meaning of computation.","Rapid technological advances have shifted this investigation from pure mathematical theory into a matter of urgent practical concern."]},{"page":4,"title":"The Evolutionary Timeline of Computing","details":["The Abstract Blueprint (1936) - Alan Turing & Alonzo Church.","The Stored-Program Architecture (1945) - John von Neumann.","The Silicon & Mass Computing Microprocessor (1971) - Federico Faggin & Ted Hoff.","Vector Processing & Supercomputing (1976) - Seymour Cray.","The Quantum Shift (1980s–Present) - Richard Feynman & David Deutsch"]},{"page":5,"title":"The Evolutionary Timeline of Computing","details":["Invented the \"Turing Machine\" and the Engineered the Intel 4004, the world’s ﬁrst Proposed computing based on quantum λ-calculus. commercial microprocessor. mechanics rather than classical physics.","They deﬁned what an \"algorithm\" is Stripped a room-sized computer's CPU onto a Replaced classical binary bits (0 or 1) with mathematically before a single digital single silicon chip smaller than a ﬁngernail, quantum bits (qubits) that exist in","computer existed shifting computers from massive enterprise superposition, threatening to break modern tools to accessible personal devices. cryptography by solving otherwise \"intractable\" problems exponentially faster.","1945 1976 1936 1971 1980 Formulated the \"Von Neumann Architecture.\" Designed the Cray-1 Supercomputer.","He introduced the concept of sharing a single memory space for both data and program It used a unique vector processing design to instructions the foundational structural execute hundreds of operations","blueprint for almost all modern CPUs today. simultaneously in parallel, paving the way for modern high-performance computational modeling."]},{"page":6,"title":"Exercise 01","details":["A quantum computer can solve a factoring problem in minutes that would take a classical supercomputer billions of years to compute. Based on this, do you think a quantum computer can solve a problem that a Turing Machine is mathematically","incapable of solving? Take 60 seconds to talk to the person next to you."]},{"page":7,"title":"All computational","details":["problems Quantum mechanics changes the speed of computation (Complexity).","Solvable by a quantum computer?? ● But it cannot alter the absolute Solvable by laws of what is logically a Turing possible to compute Machine (Computability).","A problem that is mathematically impossible for a 1930s Turing machine is equally impossible for a 2026 quantum supercomputer."]},{"page":8,"title":"What is a \"Computer\"?","details":["Real-world modern computers are far too intricate to analyse directly with a clean mathematical theory.","We strip away the physical hardware, operating systems, and lines of code to focus purely on logic.","We study simplified, mathematical blueprints of computers called computational models."]},{"page":9,"title":"Exercise 02","details":["Imagine you are programming the controller for an automated highway toll gate.","The gate starts out with the barrier arm down to prevent cars from passing through without paying.","When a driver taps their payment card, the barrier arm lifts. Once the vehicle clears the safety sensor on the other side, the arm immediately lowers again to block the next car."]},{"page":10,"title":"Exercise 02","details":["Invalid Payment Valid Payment Valid Payment Closed Open Vehicle clears"]},{"page":11,"title":"The Three Pillars of Computation","details":["The theory of computation is traditionally divided into three central areas:","Complexity, Computability, and Automata.","All three areas are deeply linked by a single, foundational question: What are the fundamental capabilities and limitations of computers?"]},{"page":12,"title":"1. Complexity Theory","details":["Classifying computational problems based on how easy or hard they are to solve.","Sorting a million numbers is computationally easy. Creating a university class schedule with thousands of conflicting room/time constraints is computationally hard and can take centuries to solve.","While we usually want problems to be easy, fields like cryptography specifically require problems to be computationally hard so that codes cannot be cracked without a secret key."]},{"page":13,"title":"Exercise 03","details":["Task A: Take a messy list of student names and alphabetize it from A to Z.","Task B: Take a list of 500 delivery packages and find the absolute shortest driving route to deliver all of them and return to the depot.","Discuss the complexity of solving the above tasks."]},{"page":14,"title":"2. Computability Theory","details":["Classifying problems by those that are solvable by an algorithm versus those that are completely unsolvable.","In the early 20th century, logicians like Gödel, Church, and Turing discovered that certain basic mathematical problems can never be solved by a computer.","There is no algorithm capable of determining whether any given mathematical statement is true or false."]},{"page":15,"title":"Exercise 04","details":["Task A: Given an encoding of a directed graph, a starting vertex s, and a target vertex t, determine whether a valid directed path exists from s to t.","Task B: Write an IDE or optimization tool that scans a massive enterprise codebase and identifies every single function, variable, or line of code that is completely unreachable (\"dead code\") under every possible runtime condition, allowing it to be safely deleted.","Discuss the computability of the above problems."]},{"page":16,"title":"Exercise 04","details":["Task C: Build an antivirus scanner that perfectly analyzes any submitted executable file and determines if it will ever execute a malicious action (like encrypting a hard drive or modifying system files).","Task D: Given a binary integer, determine whether it is prime or composite.","Discuss the computability of the above problems."]},{"page":17,"title":"3. Automata Theory","details":["Defining the exact properties of the mathematical models of computation.","To evaluate how much time a computer needs (Complexity) or whether a problem is solvable at all (Computability), we must first have a strict, error-free mathematical definition of what a computer actually is."]},{"page":18,"title":"Exercise 05","details":["You are designing the electronic control board for a simple snack vending machine. A pack of chewing gum costs exactly Rs. 20. The machine only accepts Rs. 10 coins and has no coin-return mechanism. It will ignore any extra coins once","the cost is met. Once the candy is dispensed, the machine resets.","Identify the system's operational states, the valid user inputs, draw the transition map, and compile the state table."]},{"page":19,"title":"Exercise 05","details":["Take Gum Take Gum Insert Rs. 10 Insert Rs. 10 Insert Rs. 10 0_Rupees 10_rupees Dispense Take Gum"]},{"page":20,"title":"Exercise 06","details":["You are building the user interface logic for a digital sports watch. The stopwatch has exactly two physical buttons: Button A (Start/Pause) and Button B (Reset).","The watch starts completely cleared at zero. Pressing Reset should have no effect while the timer is actively ticking.","Map out the background state behavior of this device when a user interacts with these physical buttons."]},{"page":21,"title":"Exercise 06","details":["Press B Press B Press A Press A Press A Zeroed Running Pause Press B"]},{"page":22,"title":"Exercise 07","details":["You are programming the safety control computer for a kitchen microwave oven.","The appliance starts sitting idle. To heat food, a user must explicitly Press Start (which activates the heating magnetron tube).","For safety, if a consumer physically opens the door while the system is cooking, the magnetron must instantly shut off and freeze the remaining time. Closing the door does not automatically restart cooking; the user must manually trigger Start again.","Isolate the security states, environmental events, and the ultimate logic table for this appliance."]},{"page":23,"title":"Exercise 07","details":["Open Door / Open door / Time elapsed Press Start TIme elapsed Press Start Open door Idle Cooking Pause Time elapsed Press Start"]},{"page":24,"title":"Questions?","details":["thisaraw@boffin.lk"]}],"overview":"Automata theory studies computation using deliberately simple abstract machines. The course moves from finite memory to stacks and unbounded read/write tapes, then asks whether problems are algorithmically solvable and how much computational effort they demand.","takeaway":"Computability asks what can be computed; complexity asks what can be computed efficiently.","points":[{"title":"Three central questions","body":"Automata theory describes machine models and the languages they recognize. Computability theory identifies fundamental limits, including undecidable problems. Complexity theory analyzes time and space requirements."},{"title":"Models and their memory","body":"DFA and NFA store only a finite state. PDAs add a stack. Turing machines add unbounded writable tape; the increasing memory power changes which languages can be recognized."},{"title":"Languages are sets of strings","body":"A recognition problem can be described as a language L ⊆ Σ*. A machine recognizes L if its accepted strings are exactly the elements of L."},{"title":"Historical foundations","body":"Church’s λ-calculus and Turing’s machine model emerged in the 1930s; the stored-program computer and later microprocessors implemented computation in practical hardware."}]},{"id":2,"title":"Mathematical notions","subtitle":"Sets, functions, strings, and proofs","group":"Foundations","slides":40,"sections":[{"page":2,"title":"Sets","details":["A set is a collection of objects treated as a single unit. The objects inside are called elements or members.","Examples: {56, 87, 1, 8}, {true, false}, {x, y, r, p}","The order of describing a set doesn’t matter, nor does repetition of its members.","Examples: {65, 12, 4} as same as {4, 12, 65} or {65, 12, 12, 4}","Multisets: Track element occurrences/repetition.","Examples: {7} and {7, 7} are identical as sets, but distinct as multisets"]},{"page":3,"title":"Sets","details":["Membership: e ∈ S means e is an element of S.","Non-membership: e ∉ S means e is not an element of S.","Infinite set contains infinitely many elements.","Example: The set of integers ℤ is written as {..., −2, −1, 0, 1, 2, ...}.","The set with zero members is called the empty set and is written ∅.","Set-builder/Rule notation: set containing elements according to some rule","Example: B = {n | n is an even integer}"]},{"page":4,"title":"Subsets","details":["Subset (A ⊆ B): Every element of A is also an element of B. B Proper Subset (A ⊊ B): A is a subset of B, but A A is not equal to B (there is at least one element in B that isn't in A)."]},{"page":5,"title":"Set Operations","details":["Union (A ∪ B): Everything that is in A, OR in B, or in both.","Intersection (A ∩ B): Elements that live in both A AND B simultaneously.","Complement (A): Everything in the universal domain that is not in A."]},{"page":6,"title":"Sequences and Tuples","details":["Sequence: A list of objects where order and repetition matter.","Written inside parentheses.","For example, (7, 21, 57) is not the same as (57, 7, 21).","Duplicates are respected: (7, 21, 57, 7) is a valid sequence of length 4.","Tuples: A finite sequence of length k is called a k-tuple.","A 2-tuple is specifically called an ordered pair.","Example: (q₀, a) is an ordered pair commonly used to trigger a state transition."]},{"page":7,"title":"The Power Set","details":["The Power Set of a set S, denoted as 𝒫(S) or 2ˢ, is the set of all possible subsets of S.","The Empty Set rule: The empty set ∅ is always a member of any power set.","If a finite set S has n elements, its power set 𝒫(S) will always contain exactly 2ⁿ elements.","Example: If S = {x, y}, then 𝒫(S) = {∅, {x}, {y}, {x, y}}."]},{"page":8,"title":"The Cartesian Product","details":["The Cartesian Product of two sets A and B, written as A × B, is the set of all ordered pairs where the first element belongs to A and the second belongs to B.","Formal Rule: A × B = {(x, y) | x ∈ A and y ∈ B }","Scaling up (k-sets): We can cross multiply multiple sets (A1 × A2 × ... × Ak) to construct a set of k-tuples."]},{"page":9,"title":"Exercise 01","details":["Imagine you are programming the controller for an automated highway toll gate. The gate starts out with the barrier arm down to prevent cars from passing through without paying.","When a driver taps their payment card, the barrier arm lifts. Once the vehicle clears the safety sensor on the other side, the arm immediately lowers again to block the next car.","Compute the Cartesian Product of states State × Input"]},{"page":10,"title":"Functions","details":["A function is an object that sets up an input-output relationship. A function f takes an input x and produces an output y, written as f(x) = y.","The set of all possible inputs to a function is its domain. The set of all possible allowable outputs is its codomain.","We write f: D → R to state that f is a function with domain D and codomain R."]},{"page":11,"title":"Functions","details":["Onto Functions: A function is onto if it hits every single element in its codomain (meaning its actual range equals its codomain).","Multi-Input Domain: If a function takes multiple inputs, its domain is expressed as a Cartesian product.","Example: f: A × B → C takes an ordered pair (a, b) as a single input.","Predicates: A predicate is a specific type of function whose codomain is strictly the boolean set {True, False} (or {0, 1})."]},{"page":12,"title":"Binary Relations & Equivalence","details":["Binary Relation: A predicate whose domain is the Cartesian product of two sets (A × A).","It says whether a specific relationship holds true between two elements. We often write it inline: a R b.","Equivalence Relation: A relation is an equivalence relation if it satisfies three strict criteria:","Reflexive: a R a is always true for every element.","Symmetric: If a R b is true, then b R a must be true.","Transitive: If a R b and b R s are true, then a R s must be true.","Example: The relation \"congruence modulo 7\" (written a ≡ b (mod 7)) is a classic equivalence relation."]},{"page":13,"title":"Exercise 02","details":["Consider the set of integers ℤ, and consider the relation a ≡ b (mod 3)","(meaning a and b leave the exact same remainder when divided by 3). Take 60 seconds with your neighbor and group these numbers into their matching buckets: {−3, −2, −1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}. How many distinct buckets do you get?"]},{"page":14,"title":"Graph Theory","details":["A graph G is an object consisting of two sets: a set of vertices/nodes (V) and a set of edges (E).","We write it as G = (V, E). Invalid Payment Valid Payment Undirected Graphs: The edges have no Closed Valid Payment Open directional orientation. An edge is simply an Vehicle clears unordered set of two nodes: {u, v}.","The Degree of a Node: The number of edges attached to a specific node."]},{"page":15,"title":"Directed Graphs & Self-Loops","details":["Directed Graphs (Digraphs): Edges have arrows indicating a specific direction from a source node to a target node.","An edge in a directed graph is an ordered pair (u, v) rather than a set, meaning the edge starts at u and goes to v.","In-Degree vs. Out-Degree:","In-Degree: The number of arrows entering a node.","Out-Degree: The number of arrows leaving a node.","Self-Loops: An edge can start and end at the exact same node, written as (u, u)."]},{"page":16,"title":"Paths, Cycles, and Trees","details":["Path: A sequence of nodes connected by edges. A simple path is a path that never repeats the same node twice.","Cycle: A path that starts and ends at the exact same node. A simple cycle contains no repeated nodes except for the matching start and end node.","Connected Graph: An undirected graph where a path exists between every single pair of nodes.","Tree: A connected graph that contains no simple cycles. A tree always looks like a branching hierarchy."]},{"page":17,"title":"Exercise 03","details":["Draw the directed graph G = (V, E) defined by these two sets:","V = {q₀, q₁, q₂}","E = {(q₀, q₁), (q₁, q₂), (q₂, q₂), (q₂, q₀)}"]},{"page":18,"title":"Strings and Languages","details":["Alphabet (Σ): Any finite, non-empty set of symbols.","Binary Alphabet: Σ = {0, 1}","English Alphabet: Σ = {a, b, c, …, z}","String: A finite sequence of symbols from a given alphabet, written next to each other.","Example: If Σ = {0, 1}, then 01101 is a string over Σ.","String Length (|w|): The number of symbols contained in a string w.","If w = 01101, then |w| = 5."]},{"page":19,"title":"The Empty String & Substrings","details":["The Empty String (ε): The unique string containing exactly zero symbols. Its length is always zero:","|ε| = 0.","Substrings: A string z is a substring of w if z appears consecutively anywhere inside w.","Example: If w = cadillac, then dill is a substring of w.","String Concatenation: Combining two strings x and y by pasting y onto the end of x to form a single joint string, written as xy.","Multi-concatenation power identity: xᵏ means repeating the string x exactly k times.","(Note: x⁰ = ε)."]},{"page":20,"title":"Formal \"Language\"","details":["A Language is simply a set of strings over a chosen alphabet.","The Kleene Star (Σ*): The infinite set of all possible finite strings that can ever be built using the symbols from alphabet Σ.","Mathematical Truth: A language L is a subset of the universal string space (L ⊆ Σ*)."]},{"page":21,"title":"Ordering Strings","details":["Lexicographic Ordering: The standard dictionary order where strings are sorted alphabetically based on the underlying symbol order.","Short strings can come after long strings if they start with a later letter.","Shortlex/String Ordering: The preferred ordering method in automata theory. Strings are sorted strictly by length first, and then ties are broken alphabetically.","Example over alphabet {0, 1} in Shortlex order:","ε, 0, 1, 00, 01, 10, 11, 000, 001, …"]},{"page":22,"title":"Exercise 04","details":["Let our alphabet be Σ = {0, 1}. I have defined a formal language as follows:","L = {w ∈ Σ* | w starts with 0 and has an even length}","Write down the first 4 shortest strings that belong to this language L, sorted in proper Shortlex order."]},{"page":23,"title":"Boolean Logic","details":["Boolean Logic: A mathematical system built on two core values: TRUE (1)","and FALSE (0).","Core Operations:","Negation / NOT (¬ or p̄): Flips the truth value.","Conjunction / AND (∧): True only if both inputs are true.","Disjunction / OR (∨): True if at least one input is true."]},{"page":24,"title":"Boolean Logic","details":["Advanced Structural Operators:","Exclusive OR / XOR (⊕): True if the inputs are different (one is true, one is false).","False if they are identical.","Equality / Equivalence (↔ or ≡): True if the inputs are identical (both true or both false). This is the direct logical opposite of XOR.","The Conditional / Implication (→): p → q means \"If p, then q\". It is only false when p is true but q is false."]},{"page":25,"title":"The Distributive Laws","details":["Describes how AND and OR operations interact when nested.","Law 1: AND distributes over OR a ∧ (b ∨ c) = (a ∧ b) ∨ (a ∧ c)","Law 2: OR distributes over AND a ∨ (b ∧ c) = (a ∨ b) ∧ (a ∨ c)"]},{"page":26,"title":"Exercise 05","details":["Imagine you are reviewing a code for a critical state-machine safety check. There is a massive conditional statement to evaluate three boolean flags: A (System Armed), B (Sensor High), and C (Timer Active).","The code reads:","if (A and B) or (A and C) or (A and (not B or not C)):","This translates to the formal boolean expression:","(A ∧ B) ∨ (A ∧ C) ∨ (A ∧ (¬B ∨ ¬C))","Use the Distributive Laws we just learned to refactor and radically simplify this expression. What is the absolute shortest equivalent logic?"]},{"page":27,"title":"Transitions as Logic","details":["Under the hood, an automaton's control unit is just a collection of physical logic gates (AND, OR, NOT).","Moving from one state to another is a conditional branch evaluated using boolean math.","If a machine is in State q₀ AND reads the symbol 1, the transition evaluates to TRUE and the machine jumps to the next state.","Complex routing inside a machine is just a network of these binary decisions happening simultaneously."]},{"page":28,"title":"Problems as Boolean Predicates","details":["A computer's job in this course is to look at a string w and decide if it is valid or invalid.","We model this computation as a Predicate Function (a function that outputs a boolean value).","Mathematical Mapping:","f(w) {1, 0} Output 1 (True): The string belongs to the language ACCEPT.","Output 0 (False): The string violates the rules REJECT."]},{"page":29,"title":"Definitions, Theorems, & Proofs","details":["Definition: An explicit, unambiguous specification of a mathematical object or rule (e.g., G = (V, E)).","Theorem: A mathematical statement or property claimed to be 100% true under given definitions.","Proof: A rigorous, step-by-step logical deduction demonstrating that a theorem holds true without exception."]},{"page":30,"title":"Lemmas & Corollaries","details":["Lemma: A smaller helper theorem proven before a main theorem to break complex logic into modular, readable parts.","Corollary: A bonus truth that follows naturally and immediately after a main theorem has been proven.","Lemma Theorem Corollary (Helper Tool) (Main Result) (Instant Bonus)"]},{"page":31,"title":"Why Proofs & Definitions Matter in Automata","details":["Infinite Input Spaces: Software test suites can only check finite inputs. A proof guarantees a machine works across infinitely many strings (Σ*).","Proving Impossibility: To prove a machine cannot solve a problem (e.g., validating nested HTML tags), we must use a formal proof as testing cannot prove a negative.","Algorithm Correctness: We use proofs to guarantee that machine transformations (e.g., converting NFAs to DFAs) preserve identical behavior."]},{"page":32,"title":"Exercise 06","details":["Match each statement below to its correct role: [ Definition | Lemma | Theorem | Proof | Corollary ]","A: \"An Even Group is any group whose total count N can be written as N = 2k for some integer k.\" B: \"Algebraic helper rule: 2a + 2b = 2(a + b).\" C: \"Merging any two separate Even Groups always creates a group that can be split into 2 equal teams.\"","D: \"Let Group 1 have 2a players and Group 2 have 2b players. Total = 2a + 2b = 2(a + b). Since (a + b) is an integer, the group satisfies Card A.\" E: \"Merging four separate Even Groups can also always be split into 2 equal teams.\""]},{"page":33,"title":"Proof Method 1: Proof by Construction","details":["Theorem: For every even integer n ≥ 4, there exists a 3-regular graph (a graph where every node has a 1 degree of exactly 3) containing n vertices.","Do not argue abstractly. Give an algorithm to build the 2 3 graph for any even n.","In a proof by construction, your goal is to act like a 4 software architect. When a theorem claims an object exists, you provide an algorithm that manufactures it."]},{"page":34,"title":"Proof Method 1 Proof by Construction","details":["Every node has 2 outer ring edges + 1 cross spoke edge = 3 edges total. The graph exists for all even n 4. 1","E = { {i, i+1} | 1 ≤ i ≤ n−1 } ∪ { {n, 1} } ∪ { {i, i + n/2} | 1 ≤ i ≤ n/2 }","2 4 3"]},{"page":35,"title":"Proof Method 2: Proof by Contradiction","details":["Theorem: √2 is an irrational number (it cannot be expressed as a ratio of two integers).","We assume that the theorem is false and then show that this assumption leads to an obviously false consequence, called a contradiction.","Assume the opposite is true: √2 = ab (where the fraction is reduced to its lowest terms)."]},{"page":36,"title":"Proof Method 2: Proof by Contradiction","details":["Assume Opposite: Assume √2 is rational. Therefore, √2 = a/b, where a/b is a fraction reduced to lowest terms (meaning a and b share no common factors).","Square both sides: 2 = a²/b² ⇒ a² = 2b².","Since a² = 2b², a² must be even, which means a must be even (a = 2k).","(2k)² = 2b² ⇒ 4k² = 2b² ⇒ 2k² = b².","Since b² = 2k², b² is even, which means b must also be even!","The Contradiction: If both a and b are even, the fraction a/b was not reduced to lowest terms. This contradicts our starting assumption. Therefore, √2 must be irrational."]},{"page":37,"title":"Proof Method 3: Proof by Induction","details":["Theorem: For any finite set S containing n elements, the power set 𝒫(S)","contains exactly 2n elements.","Every proof by induction consists of two parts, the basis and the induction step. Each part is an individual proof on its own. The basis proves that P(1)","is true. The induction step proves that for each i ≥ 1, if P(i) is true, then so is P(i + 1)."]},{"page":38,"title":"Proof Method 3: Proof by Induction","details":["Step 1: Base Case (n = 0):","A set with 0 elements is the empty set S = ∅.","Its power set is 𝒫(∅) = {∅}, which has 1 element.","Since 20 = 1, the base case holds true!"]},{"page":39,"title":"Proof Method 3: Proof by Induction","details":["Step 2: Inductive Step:","Assume a set with k elements has 2ᵏ subsets.","Prove for k + 1 elements: Add one new element x to the set. Every existing subset stays as it was (there are 2ᵏ of these).","Every existing subset can also be cloned by adding element x into it (another 2ᵏ subsets).","Total subsets = 2ᵏ + 2ᵏ = 2 · 2ᵏ = 2ᵏ⁺¹.","Conclusion: By induction, |𝒫(S)| = 2ⁿ for all n ≥ 0."]},{"page":40,"title":"Questions?","details":["thisaraw@boffin.lk"]}],"overview":"The notation behind automata matters: sets describe states and alphabets, Cartesian products describe transitions, and strings and languages describe what machines process. Formal proofs use precise quantifiers, functions, induction, and counterexamples.","takeaway":"Always distinguish a symbol, a string, a set of strings, and a set of states.","points":[{"title":"Sets and power sets","body":"Membership is x ∈ A; subset is A ⊆ B; proper subset means A ⊊ B. The power set 𝒫(A) contains every subset of A, including ∅ and A itself, and has 2ⁿ members when |A| = n."},{"title":"Sequences, tuples and Cartesian products","body":"Unlike sets, tuples preserve order. A × B = {(a,b) | a ∈ A, b ∈ B}; transitions frequently take such ordered pairs."},{"title":"Alphabets, strings, languages","body":"Σ is a finite alphabet; ε is the empty string and |ε| = 0. Σ* includes all finite strings, including ε; Σ⁺ excludes ε. A language L is any subset of Σ*."},{"title":"Proof discipline","body":"An existence claim needs a witness; a universal claim needs a general argument. Induction establishes a base case and an inductive step. Counterexamples disprove universal claims."}]},{"id":3,"title":"Finite automata","subtitle":"Determinism and regular languages","group":"Regular languages","slides":20,"sections":[{"page":2,"title":"Exercise 01","details":["Imagine a basic touch-sensitive flash-light. It starts OFF.","Tap it once to LOW brightness.","Tap it a second time to HIGH brightness.","Tap it a third time it turns OFF.","Your tasks;","1. Identify the States & Action and draw the state diagram","2. Generate state transition table","3. Does the lamp need to remember how many total taps it has ever received in its lifetime to work correctly"]},{"page":3,"title":"Finite Automata Behaviour:","details":["1. It processes an input string symbol by symbol, moving strictly left-to-right without backtracking.","2. It has no RAM, no stack, and no tape to write on. Its current state is the only memory it possesses.","3. For every input symbol it reads, it uses its current state to make an immediate, unambiguous jump to the next state.","4. After reading the final symbol, if the machine rests in a designated Accept State, it approves the input; otherwise, it Rejects."]},{"page":4,"title":"Formal Definition:","details":["A Deterministic Finite Automaton (DFA) is defined as a 5-tuple:","M = (Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F)","Q is a finite set of states.","Σ is a finite alphabet (a set of allowed input symbols).","δ : Q × Σ → Q is the transition function mapping a (state, symbol) pair to a new state.","q₀ ∈ Q is the start state.","F ⊆ Q is the set of accept states (also called final states)."]},{"page":5,"title":"Formal Definition:","details":["Key Notation Details to Remember:","Domain of δ: δ takes its input from the Cartesian product Q × Σ.","Transitions: Every state in Q must have an outgoing transition for every symbol in Σ.","Element vs. Subset: q₀ is a single element (q₀ ∈ Q), whereas F is a set (F ⊆ Q).","Set F: Can be empty (∅), contain one state, or contain all states."]},{"page":6,"title":"Exercise 02:","details":["We want to design a Deterministic Finite Automaton Modd over the alphabet Σ = {0, 1} that accepts a binary string if and only if it contains an odd number of 1s.","1. How many distinct memory conditions do you need to track whether the count of 1s seen so far is even or odd?","2. Draw the state diagram.","3. Write down Q, Σ, q0, F, and construct the transition table δ.","4. Trace the input string w = 1011. Does your machine end in an accept state?"]},{"page":7,"title":"Exercise 02: State Diagram and Formal Definition","details":["Formal 5-Tuple Specification:","0 0","Q = {qeven, qodd}","1","Σ = {0, 1}","qeven qodd","q₀ = qeven (0 is an even count of 1s) 1","F = {qodd}"]},{"page":8,"title":"Exercise 02: Transition Table","details":["String Trace Analysis","String: w = 1011 Current State Input 0 Input 1 r0 = qeven ↓1","r1 = qodd ↓0 qeven (Start) qeven qodd r2 = qodd ↓1","r3 = qeven ↓1 qodd (Accept) qodd qeven r4 = qodd","Result: Since final state r4 = qodd ∈ F, the string 1011 is ACCEPTED."]},{"page":9,"title":"Formal Definition of Computation","details":["Let M = (Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F) be a Deterministic Finite Automata (DFA) and let w = w₁w₂…wₙ be a string over the alphabet Σ.","The machine M accepts w if a sequence of states r₀, r₁,…, rₙ exists in Q satisfying the following three conditions:","1. Start Condition: r₀ = q₀","2. Transition Condition: δ(rᵢ, wi+1) = ri+1 for i = 0, …, n−1","3. Accept Condition: rₙ ∈ F","Rejection: If rₙ ∉ F, the machine M rejects the string w."]},{"page":10,"title":"Accept vs. Recognize","details":["Action vs. Mapping A machine accepts or rejects an individual string w.","A machine recognizes an entire language L(M).","The Language of a Machine L(M) = { w ∈ Σ* | M accepts w }","If M accepts no strings, it recognizes the empty language L(M) = ∅.","Regular Language A language A is regular if some finite automaton recognizes A (meaning A = L(M) for some DFA M)."]},{"page":11,"title":"Why the Distinction Matters in Computer Science?","details":["Acceptance is Local (Instance Level): M accepts w is an operational check on a single, finite input string w. It takes exactly |w| steps to execute.","Recognition is Global (Specification Level): M recognizes L(M) means the finite 5-tuple, its states Q and transition rules δ, captures the structural definition of the entire (often infinite) language. It provides a finite representation for an infinite set.","The Compiler Analogy: A compiler doesn't store a list of every valid code file ever written (which is infinite). Instead, the programming language's syntax is recognized by the compiler's finite parser. When a developer writes a specific code file w, the parser accepts or","rejects that single string."]},{"page":12,"title":"Exercise 03:","details":["If the given alphabet is Σ = {a, b}, design a DFA M₁ that accepts any string whose length |w| is a multiple of 3.","1. Determine the set of states Q, start state q₀, and accept set F. (Hint: Think about remainders when dividing string length by 3).","2. Sketch the state diagram and complete the transition table δ.","3. Trace the state sequence r₀, r₁, ..., r₆ for w = aaabbb. Does your machine end in F?"]},{"page":13,"title":"Exercise 04:","details":["If the given Alphabet: Σ = {0, 1}, design a DFA M₂ that accepts strings that start with 10.","Instructions:","1. Identify all states Q, start state q₀, and accept set F. (Hint: What happens if the very first symbol is 0?)","2. Sketch the state diagram and complete the transition table δ.","3. Trace state sequences for both w₁ = 1010 and w₂ = 0110. Confirm that your machine accepts w₁ and rejects w₂."]},{"page":14,"title":"Exercise 05:","details":["If the given alphabet: Σ = {0, 1}, design a DFA M₃ that accepts strings that end with 01.","1. Identify states Q needed to track progress toward 01, specify start state q₀, and accept set F.","2. Sketch the state diagram and complete the transition table δ. (Hint: What happens if you get a 0 when expecting a 1?)","3. Trace the step-by-step state sequence for w = 1001. Does it land in an accept state?"]},{"page":15,"title":"Exercise 06:","details":["If the given alphabet: Σ = {0, 1}, design a DFA M₄ that accepts strings containing the substring 11 anywhere.","1. Determine states Q, start state q₀, and accept set F. (Hint: Once 11 is found, does the machine ever need to leave the accept state?)","2. Sketch the state diagram and complete the transition table δ.","3. Trace the state sequence for w = 0110. Verify the exact step where your machine enters the accept state."]},{"page":16,"title":"Exercise 03 - Answers:","details":["The machine only needs to remember the remainder of the string Current State Input a Input b length divided by 3.","q₀ (Start/Accept) q₁ q₁ State Design:","q₁ q₂ q₂ q₀: Length mod 3 = 0 (Start state & Accept state)","q₁: Length mod 3 = 1 q₂ q₀ q₀ q₂: Length mod 3 = 2 Formal Solution:","Q = {q₀, q₁, q₂}","q₀ = q₀, F = {q₀} (empty string ε has length 0, which is a multiple of 3)","Transitions:"]},{"page":17,"title":"Exercise 04 - Answers:","details":["Current State Input 0 Input 1 The machine validates the first two characters. If a string violates qstart qdead qsaw_1 the prefix rule early, it drops into a state it can never escape.","State Design: qsaw_1 qaccept qdead qstart: Reading symbol 1 qaccept (Accept) qaccept qaccept qsaw_1: Read 1, expecting 0 qaccept: Successfully started with 10 (Accept state) qdead qdead qdead qdead: Violates prefix rule (Rejection trap)","Formal Solution:","Q = {qstart, qsaw_1, qaccept, qdead}","q0 = qstart, F = {qaccept}","Transitions:"]},{"page":18,"title":"Exercise 05 - Answers:","details":["Current State Input 0 Input 1 The machine cannot predict when the string ends, so it must always track whether the most recent qstart q₀ qstart symbols read match the target pattern 01.","State Design: q₀ q₀ q₀₁ qstart: Base state / Last symbol read was not 0 q₀: Last symbol read was 0 (halfway to target) q₀₁ (Accept) q₀ qstart q₀₁: Last two symbols read were 01 (Accept state)","Formal Solution:","Q = {qstart, q₀, q₀₁}","qstart = qstart, F = {q₀₁}","Transitions:"]},{"page":19,"title":"Exercise 06 - Answers:","details":["Current State Input 0 Input 1 Once the target pattern 11 is found anywhere in the string, the machine has succeeded and remains in qbase qbase qsaw_1 the accept state regardless of future inputs.","State Design: qsaw_1 qbase qfound qbase: Have not seen 1 yet qsaw_1: Saw a single 1 qfound (Accept) qfound qfound qfound: Saw 11 (Accept state)","Formal Solution:","Q = {qbase, qsaw_1, qfound}","q0 = qbase, F = {qfound}","Transitions:"]},{"page":20,"title":"Questions?","details":["thisaraw@boffin.lk"]}],"overview":"A deterministic finite automaton (DFA) processes an input one symbol at a time and tracks only which state it currently occupies. Its transition table or directed graph completely defines its behavior. DFAs recognize precisely the regular languages.","takeaway":"State represents the minimum finite information about the input prefix needed for future decisions.","points":[{"title":"The five-tuple","body":"M = (Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F), with finite Q, alphabet Σ, total transition δ: Q × Σ → Q, start q₀ ∈ Q, and accepting set F ⊆ Q."},{"title":"Acceptance","body":"The entire input must be consumed; accept exactly when the final state belongs to F. Entering an accepting state before the input ends does not mean the machine can stop and accept early."},{"title":"Odd number of ones","body":"Use q_even (start, nonaccepting) and q_odd (accepting). Reading 0 keeps the state; reading 1 switches parity. For 1011, the number of ones is 3, so accept."},{"title":"Regular expressions and closure","body":"Regular languages can be described by DFAs, NFAs, or regular expressions. They are closed under union, intersection, complement, concatenation, and Kleene star."},{"title":"Design a state from a memory condition","body":"For ends-with-01, the state must encode whether the current suffix is empty/unhelpful, 0, or 01. For contains-00, track whether a 0 has just been seen or the pattern already occurred."}]},{"id":4,"title":"Nondeterministic automata","subtitle":"Branching computation and ε-moves","group":"Regular languages","slides":20,"sections":[{"page":2,"title":"Deterministic Finite Automata (DFA)","details":["Everything we have built so far is a Deterministic Finite Automaton (DFA). \"Deterministic\" means every step of computation is strictly predetermined and predictable.","The 3 Rules of Determinism:","1. Exactly One Choice: For any state q and symbol a, δ(q, a) outputs exactly one next state.","2. No Missing Outlets: Every state must handle every symbol in Σ (no missing transitions).","3. No Reading ε: The machine cannot change states without consuming an input character.","For any input string w, a DFA executes a single, repeatable sequence of states from start to finish."]},{"page":4,"title":"Revisit Exercise 05:","details":["Required tracking full state memory every state needed strict, explicit transitions for both 0 and 1 to handle reset paths (e.g., handling inputs like 00 or 101).","DFA NFA 1 0,1 0 0 0 1 qs q0 qs q0 q01 1 1 0 q01"]},{"page":5,"title":"Revisit Exercise 05:","details":["qs stays in the background, looping on every NFA 0,1 symbol (0, 1).","Whenever a 0 appears, the NFA spawns a 0 1 new guess path to q0 to check if this is the qs q0 q01 start of the final 01 suffix.","If wrong, that path dies in q0. If right, it lands in q01 (Accept)."]},{"page":6,"title":"Exercise 01:","details":["Suppose you already know how to build two simple machines:","Machine A: Accepts strings containing substring 00.","Machine B: Accepts strings containing substring 11.","Design a single machine over Σ = {0, 1} that accepts a string if it satisfies Machine A OR Machine B."]},{"page":7,"title":"Exercise 01:","details":["0,1 0 0 qs0 q0 q00 ε qs 0,1 ε 1 1 qs1 q1 q11"]},{"page":8,"title":"ε-Transitions","details":["A transition labeled with ε (the empty string, where |ε| = 0).","Moving along an ε-arrow consumes 0 input characters from the input string.","The move happens automatically the instant the machine arrives at the state.","If a state has outgoing ε-transitions, the machine instantly exists in both the origin state and all target states simultaneously before reading the next character.","Allows a machine to make structural branch choices without eating or losing the first symbol of the input stream."]},{"page":9,"title":"Deterministic vs. Nondeterministic Automata","details":["Feature DFA (Deterministic) NFA (Nondeterministic)","Transitions per (State, Symbol) Exactly 1 0, 1, or multiple Empty Input Transitions (ε) Not allowed Allowed (free teleports without reading input)","Computation Path Single, linear path Tree of parallel computation paths Acceptance Condition Final state ∈ F At least one active path ends in F"]},{"page":10,"title":"Formal Definition of an NFA Critical Differences from DFAs","details":["An NFA is mathematically defined as a 5-tuple: Unlike deterministic machines, an NFA incorporates two primary mathematical formalisms:","M = (Q, Σ, δ, q0, F)","1. ε-Transitions Allowed where the components are defined as: The alphabet includes the empty string ε, allowing state Q : A finite set of states. changes without consuming input:","Σε = Σ ∪ {ε}","Σ : A finite alphabet.","δ : Q × Σε → 𝒫(Q) is the transition function.","2. Power Set Mapping q0 ∈ Q is the start state. The transition function outputs a set of states (elements of F ⊆ Q is the set of accept states. the power set 𝒫(Q)) rather than a single state:","δ : Q × Σε → 𝒫(Q)"]},{"page":11,"title":"Why NFAs Matter?","details":["Eliminates \"reset logic.\" You design for the success path and let invalid branches crash automatically.","An NFA with n states can require up to 2ⁿ states to represent as a DFA.","Makes combining machines (Union, Concatenation, Star) effortless via ε-transitions.","Real-world pattern matchers (like grep or system regex parsers) construct NFAs under the hood because they are far easier to assemble programmatically."]},{"page":12,"title":"Exercise 02:","details":["Given that the alphabet: Σ = {0, 1}, design an NFA that accepts strings where the 3rd symbol from the right is 1.","Examples: 100 (Accept), 1011 (Reject), 0101 (Accept), 10 (Reject)."]},{"page":13,"title":"Exercise 03:","details":["Given that the alphabet: Σ = {0, 1}, Design an NFA that accepts only the exact string 012."]},{"page":14,"title":"Regular Operations","details":["Let A and B be languages over an alphabet Σ.","01. Union (A ∪ B):","A ∪ B = { x | x ∈ A or x ∈ B }","Meaning: Accepts strings belonging to either language.","Construction Logic:","Create a brand-new start state q₀.","Add ε-transitions from q₀ to the original start state of N₁ and the original start state of N₂.","Keep all original accept states F = F₁ ∪ F₂."]},{"page":15,"title":"02. Concatenation (A ∘ B):","details":["A ∘ B = { xy | x ∈ A and y ∈ B }","Meaning: Accepts strings formed by taking a string from A directly followed by a string from B.","Construction Logic:","Start state of the new machine is the original start state of N₁.","Draw an ε-transition from every accept state in F₁ to the start state of N₂.","Demote F₁: The old accept states of N₁ are no longer accept states!","The only valid accept states are now F₂ (the accept states of N₂)."]},{"page":16,"title":"03. Kleene Star (A*):","details":["A* = { x₁x₂...xₖ | k ≥ 0 and each xᵢ ∈ A }","Meaning: Accepts any sequence of zero or more strings glued together from A.","(Note: k = 0 means ε ∈ A*, so the empty string is always in A*)","Construction Logic:","Create a brand-new start state q₀ and make it an accept state (this guarantees ε ∈ A*).","Add an ε-transition from q₀ to N₁'s original start state.","Add feedback ε-transitions from every accept state in F₁ back to N₁'s original start state to allow repeated loops."]},{"page":17,"title":"Exercise 04:","details":["Given Automata: 0 qs0 q0 N1 accepts L1 = {0} ε N2 accepts L2 = {1} qs Construct an NFA for L1 ∪ L2 = {0, 1}.","ε 1 qs1 q1"]},{"page":18,"title":"Exercise 05:","details":["Given Automata: ε 0 ε 1 N1 accepts L1 = {0} qs0 q0 qs1 q1 N2 accepts L2 = {1}","Construct an NFA for L1 ∘ L2 = {01}."]},{"page":19,"title":"Exercise 05:","details":["Given Automaton: N₁ accepts L₁ = {01} ε ε 0 1 Construct an NFA for qs q0 q1 q2 L₁* = {01}* = {ε, 01, 0101, 010101, ...}","ε"]},{"page":20,"title":"Questions?","details":["thisaraw@boffin.lk"]}],"overview":"An NFA may have multiple next states, no transition, or an ε-transition that consumes no input. It accepts if at least one possible computation path finishes in an accepting state after reading the entire string.","takeaway":"Nondeterminism provides convenient alternative paths, not more language-recognition power than a DFA.","points":[{"title":"Transition type","body":"Without ε-moves, δ: Q × Σ → 𝒫(Q); with ε-moves, δ: Q × (Σ ∪ {ε}) → 𝒫(Q). A transition returns a set of possible next states."},{"title":"Existential acceptance","body":"If any branch reaches a final state after consuming the input, accept. One failed branch does not make the whole NFA reject."},{"title":"ε moves","body":"An ε-transition changes state without consuming a character. ε is a zero-length string, not a literal character appearing in the input."},{"title":"Constructing union","body":"An ε-NFA can have a new start state with ε-arrows to the start states of two separate NFAs. Accepting either branch implements L₁ ∪ L₂."},{"title":"Suffix guessing","body":"For strings ending in 01, an NFA can loop over arbitrary prefixes and nondeterministically guess which 0 begins the final 01."}]},{"id":5,"title":"NFA → DFA","subtitle":"Subset construction and ε-closure","group":"Regular languages","slides":21,"sections":[{"page":2,"title":"Lecture detail · 2","details":["NFAs offer parallel execution, implicit rejection, and ε-transitions, making complex machine assembly far easier.","Does this extra flexibility allow NFAs to recognise brand-new languages that no DFA could ever recognise?","Every Non-Deterministic Finite Automaton has an equivalent Deterministic Finite Automaton.","Nondeterminism changes design convenience and state efficiency, but not absolute computational power: L(NFA) = L(DFA)."]},{"page":3,"title":"The Subset Construction","details":["A DFA must track all possible parallel execution paths of an NFA using a single deterministic state.","Each state in the new DFA represents a set of states that the NFA could simultaneously occupy after reading a given input string.","If an NFA has n states (|Q| = n), the equivalent DFA's states are drawn from the Power Set 𝒫(Q).","Maximum possible DFA states = 2ⁿ (though many subsets are often unreachable in practice).","A DFA state is an accept state if it contains at least one accept state from the original NFA."]},{"page":4,"title":"The ε-Closure Operator: E(R)","details":["For any set of NFA states R ⊆ Q, the ε-closure E(R) is the set of all states reachable from R by taking 0 or more ε-transitions.","Key Rules of E(R):","Self-Inclusion: Every state in R is automatically in E(R) (0 steps taken).","Propagation: If state q ∈ E(R) and q ─ε─► q', then q' ∈ E(R).","When to Apply E(R):","At Start: To determine the DFA start state q₀'.","After Transitions: Every time a symbol is read, instantly calculate E(R) on the resulting state set to absorb all free jumps."]},{"page":5,"title":"When to Apply E(R):","details":["Machine Initialization (Finding DFA Start State q₀')","Before reading any input symbols, calculate E({q₀}).","Captures all states reachable from original start state q₀ via zero or more ε-transitions.","Result: DFA Start State q₀' = E({q₀})","Evaluating Input Transitions (Finding Next DFA State)","Step A (Consume Symbol): Collect all immediate target states reachable on symbol 'a' from current set R:M = ⋃_{q ∈ R} δ(q, a)","Step B (Apply Closure): Instantly expand set M to include all secondary free jumps:δ'(R, a) = E(M)"]},{"page":6,"title":"Subset Construction","details":["Given an NFA M = (Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F), we construct an equivalent DFA M' = (Q', Σ, δ', q₀', F') as follows:","1. DFA States: Q' = 𝒫(Q)","2. DFA Start State: q₀' = E({q₀})","3. DFA Transition Function δ':","For any DFA state R ∈ Q' and symbol a ∈ Σ:","δ'(R, a) = E( ⋃_{q ∈ R} δ(q, a) )","4. DFA Accept States:","F' = { R ∈ Q' | R ∩ F ≠ ∅ }"]},{"page":7,"title":"Exercise 06:","details":["Given NFA M over Σ = {0, 1}:","Q = {q₀, q₁, q₂}","q₀ = q₀, F = {q₂}","Transitions:","δ(q₀, 0) = {q₀, q₁}, δ(q₀, 1) = {q₁}, δ(q₀, ε) = ∅ δ(q₁, 0) = ∅, δ(q₁, 1) = {q₂}, δ(q₁, ε) = {q₂}","δ(q₂, 0) = {q₀}, δ(q₂, 1) = ∅, δ(q₂, ε) = ∅","Tasks:","Compute E({q₀}).","Construct the transition table for the equivalent DFA.","Identify all final accept states F' in the resulting DFA."]},{"page":8,"title":"Exercise 06: Solution","details":["DFA Start State: q₀' = E({q₀}) = {q₀}","DFA State R Input 0 Input 1 Accept State?","{q₀} {q₀, q₁, q₂} {q₁, q₂} No {q₀, q₁, q₂} {q₀, q₁, q₂} {q₁, q₂} YES (contains q₂)","{q₁, q₂} {q₀} {q₂} YES (contains q₂)","{q₂} {q₀} ∅ YES (contains q₂)","∅ ∅ ∅ No"]},{"page":9,"title":"Introduction to Regular Expressions","details":["Declarative vs. Imperative:","Automata (DFAs/NFAs) describe computation by how a machine steps through state transitions.","Regular Expressions (Regex) describe computation by what pattern the language strings look like.","Modern pattern matchers in Python, JavaScript, Linux (grep, awk), and compiler lexers use regular expressions to search, validate, and parse text streams.","Every Regular Expression can be converted into an equivalent NFA, proving that Regexes describe the exact same family of Regular Languages."]},{"page":10,"title":"Formal Definition of Regular Expressions","details":["R is a regular expression over alphabet Σ if R is:","a for some a ∈ Σ (Atomic character matching)","ε (Matches the empty string)","∅ (Matches the empty language; matches no string)","(R₁ ∪ R₂) (Union: Matches strings in R₁ or R₂)","(R₁ ∘ R₂) (Concatenation: Matches a string from R₁ followed by R₂)","(R₁*) (Kleene Star: Matches 0 or more repetitions of R₁)"]},{"page":11,"title":"Operator Precedence & Common Shorthands","details":["Operator Precedence (Highest to Lowest):","Star (*) (Binds tightest)","Concatenation (∘)","Union (∪) (Lowest priority)","Example: a ∪ bc* is evaluated as a ∪ (b ∘ (c*))","Common Syntactic Sugar (Shorthands):","R⁺ = R R* (One or more repetitions)","R? = R ∪ ε (Optional element; zero or one match)","Σ = (0 ∪ 1) (Any single character in the alphabet)"]},{"page":12,"title":"Exercise 07:","details":["Let Σ = {0, 1}. Describe the language denoted by each regular expression:","0*10* (0 ∪ 1)* 11 (0 ∪ 1)* (00 ∪ 11)* (0 ∪ ε)(1 ∪ ε)","Tasks:","Take 3 minutes to write down English descriptions or example strings for each language."]},{"page":13,"title":"Exercise 08:","details":["Let Σ = {0, 1}. Write regular expressions for the following languages:","L₁ = { w | w starts with 1 and ends with 0 }","L₂ = { w | w contains an odd number of 1s }","L₃ = { w | w does not contain the substring 11 }","Tasks:","Take 4 minutes to draft these expressions."]},{"page":15,"title":"Equivalence of Regex and Automata","details":["Kleene's Theorem:","A language is recognized by a finite automaton (DFA/NFA) if and only if it can be described by a Regular Expression.","The Two Directions:","Regex ──► NFA: Convert any regex into an equivalent NFA (Structural Induction).","DFA ──► Regex: Convert any DFA into an equivalent regex (GNFA / State Elimination)."]},{"page":16,"title":"Equivalence of Regex and Automata","details":["To convert any regex R into an NFA N:","Base Cases (Atomic Expressions):","R = a: ──► (q₀) ──a──► ((q₁))","R = ε: ──► ((q₀))","R = ∅: ──► (q₀) (No transitions, no accept states)","Inductive Steps (Operators):","R₁ ∪ R₂: Apply NFA Union construction.","R₁ ∘ R₂: Apply NFA Concatenation construction.","R₁*: Apply NFA Kleene Star construction."]},{"page":18,"title":"Exercise 09:","details":["Construct NFAs for the following regular expressions using the formal inductive rules (base cases + operators):","R₁ = (0 ∪ 1)0","Focus: Combining Union followed by Concatenation.","R₂ = (01)*","Focus: Combining Concatenation wrapped in Kleene Star.","Take 4 minutes to draw the atomic sub-machines first, then glue them together using ε-transitions."]},{"page":20,"title":"Exercise 09 Solution:","details":["Solution 1: (0 ∪ 1)0 Build atomic machines for 0 and 1.","Create new start state q₀ with ε-branches to both 0 and 1 (Union).","Connect old accept states of (0 ∪ 1) via ε-arrows to the start state of the final 0 machine (Concatenation).","Solution 2: (01)* Build atomic machine for 01 by chaining 0 ─ε─► 1 via concatenation.","Add new start state S_{new} (Accept state) with an ε-arrow to the start of 01 (Handles ε).","Add a feedback ε-arrow from the end of 01 back to its start state (Handles loops)."]},{"page":21,"title":"Questions?","details":["thisaraw@boffin.lk"]}],"overview":"Subset construction replaces NFA branching with a single DFA state representing all NFA states reachable after the same prefix. ε-closure is applied before reading input and after each symbol transition.","takeaway":"One DFA state is a set of NFA states; accepting subsets contain at least one original accepting state.","points":[{"title":"ε-closure","body":"E(R) includes all states in R and all states reachable from R through zero or more ε-transitions."},{"title":"Start and transition","body":"The initial DFA state is E({q₀}). From a subset R on symbol a: δ′(R,a) = E(⋃_{q∈R} δ(q,a))."},{"title":"Accepting condition","body":"F′ = {R ⊆ Q | R ∩ F ≠ ∅}. Every reachable subset intersecting original F is accepting."},{"title":"State explosion","body":"An NFA with n states can yield up to 2ⁿ possible DFA states. Only reachable subsets need to be generated in practical construction."},{"title":"Empty subset","body":"When no NFA path remains, the DFA transitions to ∅. The empty subset is a valid nonaccepting dead state."}]},{"id":6,"title":"Pushdown automata","subtitle":"Stacks and the pumping lemma","group":"Context-free languages","slides":31,"sections":[{"page":2,"title":"Exercise 01:","details":["Design a Deterministic Finite Automaton (DFA) over the alphabet Σ = {0, 1} that accepts the following language:","B = { 0n1n | n >= 0 } = { ε, 01, 0011, 000111, 00001111, ... }","Draw a state transition diagram for a machine that accepts strings where every block of 0s is followed by an equal number of 1s.","Ensure your machine works for any arbitrary value of n (e.g., n = 1, n = 5, n = 100)."]},{"page":3,"title":"The Limits of Finite State:","details":["Recognizing strings with matching counts of 0s and 1s requires tracking an arbitrarily large number of inputs.","Fixed Finite Capacity: A Deterministic Finite Automaton possesses a fixed, finite number of states (p) by definition.","Processing any string containing p or more 0s forces the machine to revisit at least one state it has already seen.","Revisiting a state creates a loop in the machine that cannot track how many times it was traversed.","Inevitable Memory Breakdown: A finite state machine cannot distinguish between different counts of zeros once it starts looping."]},{"page":4,"title":"The Pigeonhole Principle in Automata:","details":["If you place n items into m containers and n > m, at least one container must hold more than one item.","Processing a string of length p requires a sequence of p + 1 total state visits.","Traversing p + 1 state visits using only p available states guarantees at least one state is visited twice.","Creation of Loops: The sequence of transitions between the first and second visit to that repeated state forms an inescapable loop."]},{"page":5,"title":"The Pumping Lemma Theorem","details":["Every regular language possesses a critical length p where any string of length at least p can be pumped.","If a language L is regular, there exists a constant integer p ≥ 1. For any string w ∈ L whose length is at least p (|w| ≥ p), we can split w into three contiguous pieces w = xyz such that:","y can be repeated (\"pumped\") indefinitely: xyiz ∈ L for every integer i ≥ 0.","The loop is non-empty: |y| > 0.","The loop happens early: |xy| ≤ p."]},{"page":6,"title":"Exercise 02","details":["Consider the language B = { 0n1n | n ≥ 0 } over the alphabet Σ = {0, 1}.","Assume for contradiction that language B is regular with an unknown pumping length p.","Select the specific string w = 0p1p, which belongs to B and satisfies |w| ≥ p.","Determine which characters must make up the substring y under the constraint |xy| ≤ p, and show why pumping y creates a contradiction."]},{"page":7,"title":"Exercise 02: Solution","details":["Assume B is regular with pumping length p, and choose string w = 0p1p in B where |w| ≥ p.","By the Pumping Lemma, w must split into xyz where |xy| ≤ p and |y| > 0.","Because |xy| ≤ p, the loop segment y must consist strictly of 0s.","Pumping to i = 2 yields the string xy2z, which contains more 0s than 1s and thus does not belong to B.","Accepting a string outside of B violates condition 1 of the Pumping Lemma, proving language B is nonregular."]},{"page":8,"title":"The Power of a Stack","details":["Finite state machines fail on unbounded counting because they lack a dynamic storage mechanism.","Adding a Last-In, First-Out (LIFO) stack provides the unbounded auxiliary memory required for tracking.","A machine can push symbols onto the stack while reading 0s and pop them off while reading 1s.","Matching succeeds if the stack becomes completely empty at the exact moment the input string ends.","Incorporating a stack memory structure transforms a Deterministic Finite Automaton into a Pushdown Automaton."]},{"page":10,"title":"How a Stack Solves 0n1n","details":["Read incoming 0 symbols from the input stream and push one marker onto the stack for each occurrence.","Switch from the pushing phase to the popping phase as soon as the first 1 symbol is encountered.","Read incoming 1 symbols from the input stream and pop one marker off the stack for each occurrence.","Reject the input string immediately if a 1 arrives when the stack is already empty.","Accept the input string if the stack becomes empty at the precise end of the input string."]},{"page":11,"title":"Formal Definition of a PDA","details":["A Pushdown Automaton (PDA) is defined mathematically as a 6-tuple:","(Q, Σ, Γ, δ, q0, F)","Components:","Q: A finite set of states Σ: A finite set representing the input alphabet Γ: A finite set representing the stack alphabet δ: The transition function, defined as:","δ : Q × Σε × Γε → 𝒫 (Q × Γε)","q0 ∈ Q: The start state F ⊆ Q: The set of accept (or final) states","Note: Σε = Σ ∪ {ε} and Γε = Γ ∪ {ε}. The power set 𝒫(Q × Γε) allows for nondeterministic transitions."]},{"page":12,"title":"Formal Definition of a PDA","details":["The new symbol Γ (Gamma) represents the finite stack alphabet, dictating exactly which symbols can be pushed or popped.","The transition function δ evaluates your current state, the next input symbol, and the top stack symbol to determine the next move.","The transition function outputs a set of possible next steps because general PDAs are fundamentally nondeterministic."]},{"page":13,"title":"PDA Transition Mechanics and Notation","details":["Taking a transition requires matching the current state, reading a specific input symbol, and successfully popping a specific top stack symbol.","Two-Part Actions: The result of executing a transition specifies the new state to enter and the new symbol to push onto the stack.","Transitions are typically written on state diagram arrows using the shorthand format \"input, pop → push\".","A machine can use ε to intentionally skip reading an input symbol, skip popping a stack symbol, or skip pushing a stack symbol.","Machines traditionally begin by pushing a special bottom-of-stack marker (like $) to easily detect when the stack is empty later."]},{"page":14,"title":"Exercise 03","details":["Design a Pushdown Automaton (PDA) M that recognizes the language L = { 0ⁿ 1ⁿ | n ≥ 0 }.","Show its state transition diagram and its formal 6-tuple definition.","Notation Convention: Each transition arrow is labeled as a, b → c, where:","a ∈ Σε : The input symbol read from the input tape.","b ∈ Γε : The symbol popped from the top of the stack.","c ∈ Γε : The symbol pushed onto the top of the stack."]},{"page":15,"title":"Exercise 03: Solution 0, ε → 0 1, 0 → ε","details":["The PDA M is formally defined as M ε, ε → $ ε = (Q, Σ, Γ, δ, q1, F), where: ε,ε→ε ε,$→ε q0 q1 q2 q3","Q = { q0, q1, q2, q3 }","Σ = { 0, 1 }","Γ = { 0, $ }","q0 ∈ Q is the start state","F = { q3 }"]},{"page":16,"title":"Exercise 03: Solution","details":["Current State Input (a) Pop (b) Next State Push (c) Formal Notation δ(q,a,b)","q0 ε ε q1 $ δ(q0, ε, ε) = { (q1, $) }","q1 0 ε q1 0 δ(q1, 0, ε) = { (q1, 0) }","q1 1 0 q2 ε δ(q1, 1, 0) = { (q2, ε) }","q1 ε $ q3 ε δ(q1, ε, $) = { (q3, ε) }","q2 1 0 q2 ε δ(q2, 1, 0) = { (q2, ε) }","q2 ε $ q3 ε δ(q2, ε, $) = { (q3, ε) }"]},{"page":17,"title":"Exercise 04","details":["Design a Pushdown Automaton (PDA) M that accepts the language L ₁ = { 0ⁿ 1²ⁿ | n ≥ 0 }.","Provide both the state transition diagram and the formal 6-tuple definition."]},{"page":18,"title":"Exercise 04: Solution","details":["The PDA M is formally defined as","M = (Q, Σ, Γ, δ, q1, F), where:","0, ε → aa 1, a → ε","Q = { q0,q1,q2,q3}","ε, ε → $ ε","Σ = { 0, 1 } ε,ε→ε ε,$→ε q0 q1 q2 q3","Γ = { a, $ }","q0 ∈ Q is the start state","F = { q3}"]},{"page":19,"title":"Exercise 05","details":["Design a Pushdown Automaton (PDA) that accepts the language L2 = { wwR | w ∈ {a, b}* }, where wR is the reverse of string w. Provide both the state transition diagram and the formal 6-tuple definition."]},{"page":20,"title":"Exercise 05: Solution","details":["The PDA M is formally defined as a, ε → a","M = (Q, Σ, Γ, δ, q1, F), where: b, ε → b a, a → ε b, b → ε","Q = { q0,q1,q2,q3}","ε, ε → $ ε","Σ = { a, b } ε,ε→ε ε,$→ε q0 q1 q2 q3","Γ = { a, b, $ }","q0 ∈ Q is the start state","F = { q3}"]},{"page":21,"title":"Exercise 06","details":["Design a Pushdown Automaton for L1 = { anb(n+m)cm | n, m >= 0 }. Provide both the state transition diagram and the formal 6-tuple definition."]},{"page":22,"title":"Exercise 06: Solution b, a → ε","details":["a, ε → a The PDA M is formally defined as ε","M = (Q, Σ, Γ, δ, q1, F), where: ε, ε → $ ε,ε→ε q0 q1 q2 b , $ → b$ b, ε → b","Q = { q0,q1,q2,q3 ,q4,q5}","Σ = { a, b, c} c, b → ε q3","Γ = { a, b, $ } ε,$→ε q5 q4 ε,ε→ε q0 ∈ Q is the start state","F = { q5}"]},{"page":23,"title":"Exercise 07","details":["Design a Pushdown Automaton for L2 = { w c wR | w in {a, b}* }. Use the explicit center symbol 'c' to trigger the state transition without nondeterministic guessing. Provide both the state transition diagram and the formal 6-tuple definition."]},{"page":24,"title":"Exercise 07: Solution","details":["The PDA M is formally defined as a, ε → a","M = (Q, Σ, Γ, δ, q1, F), where: b, ε → b a, a → ε b, b → ε","Q = { q0,q1,q2,q3}","ε, ε → $ ε","Σ = { a, b, c} c,ε→ε ε,$→ε q0 q1 q2 q3","Γ = { a, b, $ }","q0 ∈ Q is the start state","F = { q3}"]},{"page":25,"title":"Exercise 08","details":["Design a Pushdown Automaton for L = { wxwR | w in {a, b}*, x in {a, b} }. Provide both the state transition diagram and the formal 6-tuple definition."]},{"page":26,"title":"Exercise 08: Solution","details":["The PDA M is formally defined as a, ε → a","M = (Q, Σ, Γ, δ, q1, F), where: b, ε → b a, a → ε b, b → ε","Q = { q0,q1,q2,q3}","ε a,a→ε ε, ε → $","Σ = { a, b} b,b→ε ε,$→ε q0 q1 q2 q3","Γ = { a, b, $ }","q0 ∈ Q is the start state","F = { q3}"]},{"page":27,"title":"Exercise 09","details":["Design a Pushdown Automaton for L3 = { aibjck | i = j or j = k }. Provide both the state transition diagram and the formal 6-tuple definition."]},{"page":28,"title":"b,a→ε","details":["c, ε → ε Exercise 09: Solution a, ε → a The PDA M is formally defined as q2 q3 q4","M = (Q, Σ, Γ, δ, q1, F), where: ε,ε→ε ε,$→ε","Q = { q0,q1,q2,q3,q4,q5,q6,q7,q8 } ε,ε→ε ε, ε → $","Σ = { a, b, c } q0 qε1 q8","Γ = { a, b, $ } ε,ε→ε b,ε→b a, ε → ε ε,$→ε q0 ∈ Q is the start state","F = { q4, q8} q5 q6 q7 ε,ε→ε ε,ε→ε c, b → ε"]},{"page":29,"title":"Exercise 10","details":["Design a Pushdown Automaton for","L = { w in {0, 1}* | number of 0s equals number of 1s in any order }","Use the stack to store the current surplus of whichever symbol has appeared more frequently so far."]},{"page":30,"title":"Exercise 10: Solution","details":["0, $ → 0$ The PDA M is formally defined as M = (Q, Σ, Γ, 1, $ → 1$ δ, q1, F), where: 0, 0 → 00 1, 1 → 11 0, 1 → ε","Q = { q0, q1, q2 } 1, 0 → ε","Σ = { 0, 1 } ε, ε → $ ε ε,$→ε q0 q1 q2","Γ = { 0, 1, $ }","q0 ∈ Q is the start state","F = { q2 }"]},{"page":31,"title":"Questions?","details":["thisaraw@boffin.lk"]}],"overview":"A stack expands a finite automaton with potentially unbounded last-in-first-out memory. It can recognize matching-count patterns such as 0ⁿ1ⁿ that no finite automaton recognizes. The regular pumping lemma proves some languages require this additional memory.","takeaway":"The regular pumping lemma is a necessary condition for regularity, useful for disproving regularity—not proving it.","points":[{"title":"Why 0ⁿ1ⁿ is not regular","body":"A finite-state machine cannot store an arbitrary number of 0s and later compare it with the number of 1s. A PDA pushes on 0 and pops on 1."},{"title":"Regular pumping lemma","body":"If L is regular, there is p ≥ 1 such that every w ∈ L with |w| ≥ p can be decomposed w = xyz with |xy| ≤ p, |y| > 0, and xyⁱz ∈ L for every i ≥ 0."},{"title":"Proof for 0ⁿ1ⁿ","body":"Choose w = 0ᵖ1ᵖ. Since |xy| ≤ p, y is nonempty and consists entirely of 0s; pumping down i = 0 changes the zero count but not the one count."},{"title":"PDA operation","body":"Transitions can consume a symbol or ε, inspect a stack-top symbol, and replace it with a (possibly empty) string of stack symbols."},{"title":"Acceptance conventions","body":"PDAs can accept by final state or by empty stack, with conventions specified precisely; complete input consumption is required. NPDA and DPDA do not recognize exactly the same class of languages."},{"title":"Stack bottom marker","body":"Z₀ is a designated symbol marking the initial bottom of the stack, not the empty string ε. Whether it is popped depends on the PDA’s design."}]},{"id":7,"title":"Context-free languages","subtitle":"Grammars, derivations, and parse trees","group":"Context-free languages","slides":21,"sections":[{"page":2,"title":"Lecture detail · 2","details":["A Context-Free Language (CFL) is a language generated by a Context-Free Grammar (CFG)","or, equivalently, recognized by a Pushdown Automaton (PDA).","Where finite automata and regular expressions focus on scanning inputs sequentially, grammars shift the paradigm to generating strings through structural substitution rules.","Context-Free Languages include all the regular languages and many additional languages.","Context-free Regular Languages Languages"]},{"page":3,"title":"Context-Free Grammar","details":["A Context-Free Grammar is a set of recursive production rules used to construct valid strings in a language. Formally, a CFG is defined as a 4-tuple:","G = (V, Σ, R, S)","V (Variables / Non-terminals): A finite set of syntactic placeholders (e.g., S, A, B or ⟨EXPR⟩) that are replaced during string generation.","Σ (Terminals): A finite set of actual output symbols that make up the final strings (e.g., {0, 1} or {a, b, +, ×}). V ∩ Σ = ∅.","R (Production Rules): A finite set of mapping rules of the form:","A → w where A ∈ V and w ∈ (V ∪ Σ)* S ∈ V (Start Variable): The initial variable from which derivations begin."]},{"page":4,"title":"Context-Free Grammar","details":["Example: Generating 0n1n","Variables: V = {S}","Terminals: Σ = {0, 1}","Rules (R): S → 0S1 | ε Derivation for 0011:","S ⇒ 0S1 ⇒ 0(0S1)1 = 00S11 ⇒ 00(ε)11 = 0011 Why is it Called \"Context-Free\"?","The name Context-Free comes from the left-hand side constraint of the production rules:","Single Variable A → Anything w In a context-free grammar, whenever you see the variable A, you can replace it with w regardless of what symbols appear around A. The surrounding \"context\" plays zero role in whether the rule applies."]},{"page":5,"title":"Property Regular Languages Context-Free Languages","details":["Recognizing Machine Finite Automaton (DFA / NFA) Pushdown Automaton (PDA)","Generative Grammar Regular / Right-Linear Grammar Context-Free Grammar (CFG)","Memory Capability Finite (State memory only) Unbounded LIFO (Stack memory)","Example (01)* 0n1n Nondeterminism Power {NFA} ={DFA} (Equal power) {DPDA} ≄ {NPDA} (Strictly unequal)","Practical Application Lexical Analysis (Tokens/Regex) Syntax Analysis (Parsers/ASTs)"]},{"page":6,"title":"Exercise 01","details":["Consider the context-free grammar with rules","S → (S)","S→S+S S→a Construct the derivation and parse tree for the valid input string \"(a + a)\".","Substitute the start variable iteratively from left to right until all non-terminals are fully resolved.","Build a hierarchical tree where internal nodes represent non-terminal variables and leaf nodes contain terminal symbols."]},{"page":7,"title":"Exercise 01: Solution 2. Parse Tree Construction","details":["The parse tree shows the visual structure created by the","1. Leftmost Derivation for w = (a + a) grammar used to construct Abstract Syntax Trees (ASTs). ⇒ To derive the string, we replace the leftmost variable at S every step:","S ⇒ (S) (Apply Rule 1)","⇒ (S + S) (Apply Rule 2 to S) ( S )","⇒ (a + S) (Apply Rule 3 to 1st S)","⇒ (a + a) (Apply Rule 3 to 2nd S)","S + S a a Leaves (L to R): ( , a , + , a , ) &rightarrow; yields (a + a)"]},{"page":8,"title":"Exercise 01: Solution","details":["Step Stack Top Input Symbol Parser Action Applied Rule / Result 1 S ( Expand variable S S → (S) (Push ), S, ()","2 ( ( Match & Pop terminal Input ( matched and consumed 3 S a Expand variable S S → S + S (Push S, +, S)","4 S a Expand variable S S → a (Push a)","5 a a Match & Pop terminal Input a matched and consumed 6 + + Match & Pop terminal Input + matched and consumed 7 S a Expand variable S S → a (Push a)","8 a a Match & Pop terminal Input a matched and consumed 9 ) ) Match & Pop terminal Input ) matched and consumed 10 $ $ Accept Stack and input empty!"]},{"page":9,"title":"Exercise 02","details":["Consider the context-free grammar with rules S→T+S S→T T→a Perform the derivation and trace the top-down parser for the valid input string \"a + a + a\"."]},{"page":10,"title":"3. Parse Tree Construction","details":["Exercise 02: Solution","1. Formal Grammar Definition S","G = ({S, T}, {a, +}, R, S)","R: S T + S | T T a T + S","2. Leftmost Derivation for w = a + a + a S ⇒ T + S (Apply Rule 1) a T + S ⇒ a + S (Apply Rule 3 to T)","a T ⇒ a + T + S (Apply Rule 1 to S)","⇒ a + a + S (Apply Rule 3 to T)","a ⇒ a + a + T (Apply Rule 2 to S)","⇒ a + a + a (Apply Rule 3 to T) Yield (Leaves L to R): a , + , a , + , a yields a + a + a"]},{"page":11,"title":"Step Stack Top Input Symbol Parser Action Applied Rule / Result","details":["1 S a Expand variable S S → T + S (Push S, +, T)","2 T a Expand variable T T → a (Push a)","3 a a Match & Pop terminal Input a matched and consumed 4 + + Match & Pop terminal Input + matched and consumed 5 S a Expand variable S S → T + S (Lookahead sees more input)","6 T a Expand variable T T → a (Push a)","7 a a Match & Pop terminal Input a matched and consumed 8 + + Match & Pop terminal Input + matched and consumed 9 S a Expand variable S S → T (Final term in stream)","10 T a Expand variable T T → a (Push a)","11 a a Match & Pop terminal Input a matched and consumed 12 $ $ Accept Stack and input empty!"]},{"page":12,"title":"Grammar Ambiguity","details":["A context-free grammar is ambiguous if at least one string in its language has two or more distinct leftmost derivations.","Multiple derivations for a single input string produce distinctly shaped parse trees representing conflicting structural interpretations.","Compilers assign semantic meaning directly from parse tree shapes, so structural ambiguity creates undefined computation behavior.","Arithmetic Precedence Failure: An unconstrained expression grammar generates competing parse trees that evaluate addition before multiplication or vice versa.","Ambiguous grammars must be restructured into unambiguous forms before they can be used in reliable compiler parsers."]},{"page":13,"title":"Grammar Ambiguity","details":["1. Ambiguous Grammar (G1): E → E + E | E × E | id","2. Target String: w = id1 + id2 × id3","3. Two Competing Structural Parse Trees Tree A: (id1 + id2) * id3 [INCORRECT] Tree B: id1 + (id2 * id3) [CORRECT]","E E E * E E + E E + E E * E id1 id2 id3 id1 id2 id3"]},{"page":14,"title":"Grammar Ambiguity: Disambiguating Expression Grammars","details":["Precedence Level Stratification: Separate non-terminal variables into hierarchical layers to enforce strict operator evaluation order.","Top Expression Layer: Define top-level expression rules to process lowest-precedence operators like addition and subtraction.","Intermediate Term Layer: Define term-level rules to handle higher-precedence operators like multiplication and division.","Atomic Factor Layer: Enforce factor-level rules to handle atomic variables, constant numbers, and parenthesized expressions."]},{"page":15,"title":"Grammar Ambiguity: Disambiguating Expression Grammars","details":["Associativity Control: Position non-terminals recursively on the left or right side of rules to mandate evaluation direction.","The Stratified Unambiguous Grammar (G2):","E → E + T | T (Expression layer: handles +, lowest precedence)","T → T ✕ F | F (Term layer: handles , higher precedence)","F → (E) | id (Factor layer: handles atomic values and parens)"]},{"page":16,"title":"Grammar Ambiguity: Disambiguating Forced Unique Parse Tree (Grammar G₂) [UNAMBIGUOUS]","details":["Expression Grammars E Re-parsing target string: w = id₁ + id₂ ✕ id₃ Grammar G₂ yields a unique leftmost derivation:","E + T E ⇒ E + T ⇒ T + T ⇒ F + T ⇒ id₁ + T T T ✕ F ⇒ id₁ + T ✕ F ⇒ id₁ + F ✕ F ⇒ id₁ + id₂ ✕ F F F ⇒ id₁ + id₂ ✕ id₃ id₁ id₂ id₃"]},{"page":17,"title":"Equivalence of CFGs and PDAs","details":["A language is context-free if and only if some pushdown automaton recognizes it.","Every context-free grammar can be converted into an equivalent PDA and vice versa.","A PDA simulates a CFG by predicting production rules and matching terminal symbols against input.","The conversion algorithm constructs a 3-state PDA that performs all rule expansions on a single central state.","This equivalence guarantees that every grammar-based programming language can be parsed using stack-based execution."]},{"page":18,"title":"Deterministic PDAs and Compiler Parsing","details":["Unlike finite automata where DFAs match NFAs, deterministic pushdown automata are strictly less powerful than nondeterministic pushdown automata.","Deterministic Context-Free Languages form a strict subset of context-free languages that can be recognized without nondeterministic guessing.","Arbitrary context-free grammars require cubic O(n3) time algorithms such as CYK or Earley parsing to process input strings.","Programming language compilers require linear O(n) parsing time to process large source code files efficiently.","Compiler generators restrict grammars to deterministic subclasses like LL(k) or LR(k) to eliminate backtracking completely."]},{"page":19,"title":"The Language Inclusion Hierarchy","details":["Regular Languages ⊊ Deterministic CFLs (DCFL) ⊊ Context-Free Languages (CFL)","Context-Free Languages (NPDA / CFG)","e.g., wwR, aibjck (i=j or j=k)","Deterministic Context-Free Languages (DPDA / DCFL)","e.g., wcwR, 0n1n, Programming Languages Regular Languages (DFA / NFA / Regex)","e.g., (a|b)*abb, identifiers, regular expressions"]},{"page":20,"title":"Deterministic PDAs and Compiler Parsing","details":["Finite Automata: DFA = NFA Nondeterminism provides convenience, not additional language recognition power.","Pushdown Automata: DPDA ⊊ NPDA Nondeterminism provides fundamental structural power.","L₁ = { w c wʳ | w ∈ {a,b}* } ∈ DCFL (Recognized by DPDA because center marker c unambiguously dictates when to transition.)","L₂ = { w wʳ | w ∈ {a,b}* } ∈ CFL∖DCFL (Requires an NPDA because guessing the midpoint is fundamentally non-deterministic.)"]},{"page":21,"title":"Questions?","details":["thisaraw@boffin.lk"]}],"overview":"Context-free grammars generate strings through recursive replacement rules. They are equivalent in expressive power to nondeterministic PDAs and naturally express nested program syntax, arithmetic expressions, and balanced delimiters.","takeaway":"A context-free grammar generates strings; a PDA recognizes them. Both characterize the context-free languages.","points":[{"title":"The four-tuple","body":"G = (V, Σ, R, S): nonterminals V, terminals Σ, productions R, start variable S ∈ V; V and Σ are disjoint."},{"title":"Rule shape","body":"Each production is A → α, where A is a single nonterminal and α ∈ (V ∪ Σ)*. It can be applied independently of surrounding symbols."},{"title":"Example derivation","body":"S → 0S1 | ε generates 0ⁿ1ⁿ. For 0011: S ⇒ 0S1 ⇒ 00S11 ⇒ 0011."},{"title":"Parse trees","body":"A derivation shows a sequence of rule applications; a parse tree represents hierarchical syntax. Ambiguous grammars allow more than one distinct parse tree for the same string."},{"title":"The CFL pumping lemma","body":"For sufficiently long s ∈ L, s = uvwxy, |vwx| ≤ p, |vx| > 0, and uvⁱwxⁱy ∈ L for every i ≥ 0."},{"title":"Limits","body":"The language {aⁿbⁿcⁿ | n ≥ 0} is not context-free. Regular languages form a proper subset of context-free languages."}]},{"id":8,"title":"Turing machines","subtitle":"The CFL boundary and general computation","group":"Computability","slides":6,"sections":[{"page":2,"title":"Non-Context-Free Languages and the Pumping Lemma","details":["Every context-free language possesses a pumping length p such that any string s with length at least p can be partitioned into five parts s = uvwxy.","The combined middle substring vwx must have length at most p, and substrings v and x cannot both be empty (|vx| >= 1).","For all non-negative integers i, the pumped string uviwxiy must remain inside the context-free language.","Assume the target language is context-free, select a representative string s, and demonstrate that every valid partition uvwxy fails for some value of i.","Languages requiring simultaneous matching across three independent blocks fail the lemma because a pushdown stack cannot track three separate counts."]},{"page":3,"title":"Exercise 01","details":["Assume L = { anbncn | n ≥ 0 } is context-free. Let p be its pumping length given by the CFL Pumping Lemma.","Select s = apbpcp ∈ L. Note that |s| = 3p ≥ p.","By Condition 2 of the Pumping Lemma, |vwx| ≤ p. Because vwx has a maximum length of p, it cannot contain all three symbol types (a, b, and c). It can contain at most two distinct symbol types:","Case 1: vwx consists entirely of a's, entirely of b's, or entirely of c's.","Case 2: vwx spans the boundary between a's and b's (contains only a's and b's).","Case 3: vwx spans the boundary between b's and c's (contains only b's and c's)."]},{"page":4,"title":"Exercise 01 (Continued)","details":["Consider s′ = uv²wx²y:","Since |vx| ≥ 1, pumping v and x increases the count of whichever symbols appear in v and x.","In Case 1: Only one symbol count increases (e.g., more a's, but b's and c's remain p).","In Case 2 or Case 3: At most two symbol counts increase, leaving the third symbol count equal to p.","In all cases: The numbers of a's, b's, and c's in s′ are no longer equal.","Therefore, uv²wx²y ∉ L, contradicting the Pumping Lemma. L is not context-free."]},{"page":5,"title":"Introduction to Turing Machines","details":["A Turing Machine uses an infinitely long memory tape that serves as both input medium and unlimited read/write storage.","The read/write head can scan individual tape cells, overwrite symbols, and move freely to the left or right.","A Turing Machine is defined as a 7-tuple containing states, input alphabet, tape alphabet, transition function, start state, accept state, and reject state.","Entering either the accept state or the reject state causes the machine to halt its computation immediately.","The deterministic transition function maps a current state and scanned symbol to a new state, a replacement symbol, and a head movement direction."]},{"page":6,"title":"Turing Machines: Formal Definition","details":["M = (Q, Σ, Γ, δ, q₀, qaccept, qreject)","Q: Finite set of states.","Σ: Input alphabet (does not contain the blank symbol ⊔).","Γ: Tape alphabet, where Σ ⊂ Γ and ⊔ ∈ Γ.","δ: Q × Γ → Q × Γ × {L, R}: Transition function.","q₀ ∈ Q: Start state.","qaccept ∈ Q: Halting accept state.","qreject ∈ Q: Halting reject state (qaccept ≠ qreject)."]}],"overview":"The transition from PDAs to Turing machines introduces general read/write computation. The lecture first demonstrates a language beyond the context-free family with the CFL pumping lemma, preparing for machines capable of more general bookkeeping.","takeaway":"A Turing machine has finite control plus an unbounded writable tape and a movable head.","points":[{"title":"CFL pumping proof","body":"Choose aᵖbᵖcᵖ; with |vwx| ≤ p, the pumped zone cannot cover all three full-length blocks. Pumping v and x cannot preserve all three equal counts."},{"title":"TM definition","body":"A standard deterministic TM is often described by (Q, Σ, Γ, δ, q₀, q_accept, q_reject), with input alphabet Σ, tape alphabet Γ, and a blank symbol in Γ."},{"title":"Transition semantics","body":"δ(q,a) = (q′,b,D) reads a symbol, writes b, changes state to q′, and moves the head left or right (or stay when that model permits)."},{"title":"Recognition vs decision","body":"A recognizer accepts members of a language but may loop on nonmembers. A decider halts and either accepts or rejects every input."},{"title":"Computation and hierarchy","body":"A TM can recognize languages requiring more than a stack, but even a TM cannot decide every conceivable language."}]},{"id":9,"title":"Turing machine variants","subtitle":"Multitape and nondeterministic machines","group":"Computability","slides":20,"sections":[{"page":2,"title":"Variant 1: Multitape Turing Machines (k-Tape TMs)","details":["A Multitape Turing Machine consists of a finite control connected to k distinct, infinite tapes.","Each tape has its own read/write head that can move independently left, right, or stay put (S).","Formal Transition Function: Instead of reading and writing a single symbol, the transition reads a tuple of k symbols and writes a tuple of k symbols:","δ : Q × Γk → Q × Γk × {L, R, S}k Example Rule: δ(q0, a, ⊔) = (q1, X, b, R, L)","(In state q0, if tape 1 sees 'a' and tape 2 sees '⊔', switch to state q1, replace tape 1's 'a' with 'X' and move right, replace tape 2's '⊔' with 'b' and move left)."]},{"page":3,"title":"Variant 1: Multitape Turing Machines (k-Tape TMs)","details":["Simulation Theorem: Every k-tape Turing Machine M has an equivalent 1-tape Turing Machine S.","How a 1-Tape TM Simulates k Tapes: The single-tape simulator S represents all k tapes on one single tape by separating them with a special boundary symbol (#). To track where the k independent tape heads are currently located, S uses dotted/marked","versions of tape symbols (ȧ, ḃ, ⊔.).","Multitape TMs run faster (O(n) time vs O(n²) on a 1-tape TM for problems like string copying/palindromes), but they do not recognize any new languages. They only improve operational efficiency."]},{"page":4,"title":"Exercise 01","details":["Target Language: L = { aⁿbⁿcn | n ≥ 1 }","Exercise Objectives:","Design a 2-Tape Deterministic Turing Machine (M₂) that decides L.","Design a standard 1-Tape Deterministic Turing Machine (M₁) that decides L.","Compare the time complexity and design mechanics of both approaches."]},{"page":5,"title":"Exercise 01: Solution","details":["Instead of erasing or marking anything, Tape 2 acts as a reusable ruler:","Phase 1 (Copy a's): Read a's on Tape 1 and copy them onto Tape 2, moving both heads Right (R, R).","Phase 2 (Match b's): When Tape 1 hits b, shift Tape 2 left by one cell (S, L). Then read b's on Tape 1 while walking Tape 2 backward (Left) over the a's. When Tape 2 hits the blank to the left of the a's, you have verified #a = #b.","Phase 3 (Match c's): Shift Tape 2 right by one cell (S, R). Then read c's on Tape 1 while walking Tape 2 forward (Right) over the exact same a's. When Tape 1 hits ⊔ and Tape 2 hits ⊔ simultaneously, you have verified #a = #c."]},{"page":7,"title":"Exercise 01: Solution","details":["Metric 1-Tape TM (M₁) 2-Tape TM (M₂)","Language Decidability (aⁿbⁿcⁿ) Decidable (L ∈ R) Decidable (L ∈ R)","Time Complexity O(n²) (Quadratic time) O(n) (Linear time)","Active States (Excluding Halting) 5 states (q₀, q₁, q₂, q₃, q₄) 3 states (q₀, q₁, q₂)","Auxiliary Tape Markers 3 replacement markers (Γ requires X, Y, Z) Zero markers (Γ only requires {a, b, c, ⊔})","Continuous back-and-forth shuttling (n round Head Movement Pattern trips across input) Single-pass sequential sweeps (no zigzagging)","Destructively overwrites input symbols Non-destructive: keeps input on Tape 1 intact Tape Manipulation cell-by-cell while using Tape 2 as a temporary count ruler Requires O(t²) steps when converted down to a Simulation Cost Base execution model 1-tape simulator"]},{"page":8,"title":"Variant 2: Nondeterministic Turing Machines (NTMs)","details":["In an NTM, at any step, the machine can have multiple possible next moves. The computation branches into a tree of configurations.","Formal Transition Function: The transition function maps a state and symbol pair to a set of possible choices:","δ : Q × Γ → 𝒫(Q × Γ × {L, R})","An NTM accepts an input string w if at least one branch in its computation tree reaches qaccept."]},{"page":9,"title":"Variant 2: Nondeterministic Turing Machines (NTMs)","details":["Simulation Theorem: Every Nondeterministic Turing Machine N has an equivalent Deterministic Turing Machine D. Root: Config 1 How a Deterministic TM Simulates an NTM: The deterministic simulator D explores the computation tree of N using a 3-Tape","Deterministic TM: Choice 1 Choice 2 Tape 1 (Input Tape): Holds original input string w (read-only master copy).","Tape 2 (Simulation Tape): Working copy of N's tape for 1, 1 1, 2 2, 1 2, 2 running a single branch of computation.","Tape 3 (Address Tape): Tracks D's location in N's computation tree (e.g., \"231\" = choice 2 at step 1, choice 3 at step 2, choice 1 at step 3)."]},{"page":10,"title":"Variant 2: Nondeterministic Turing Machines (NTMs)","details":["The Critical Algorithm Choice: BFS vs. DFS Why Depth-First Search (DFS) FAILS: If D uses DFS, it could follow an infinite non-accepting branch forever, missing an accepting state sitting just 2 steps away on another branch.","Why Breadth-First Search (BFS) WORKS: D generates addresses in order of length (1, 2, …, n, then 11, 12, …). BFS guarantees that if an accepting configuration exists at finite depth, D will eventually find it and halt.","Nondeterminism allows exponential parallel search, but it does not expand decidability. Every NTM language is still decidable/recognizable by a deterministic TM.","This equivalence forms the exact foundation for P vs NP."]},{"page":11,"title":"Variant 3: Enumerators","details":["An Enumerator is a Turing Machine equipped with an attached printer output device. It starts with a blank tape and continuously runs, printing out strings one by one.","Turing Control Print String Output Printer (Blank Tape) \"0\", \"11\", \"010\" The Equivalence Theorem","Theorem: A language L is Turing-recognizable if and only if some Enumerator enumerates L."]},{"page":12,"title":"Variant 3: Enumerators","details":["Equivalence Logic (Directional Breakdown)","⇒ Direction (If L is Recognizable, an Enumerator exists):","Let M recognize L, and let s₁, s₂, s₃, … be all possible strings in Σ*. To handle infinite loops, the Enumerator runs M using dovetailing:","Step 1: Run M on s₁ for 1 step.","Step 2: Run M on s₁, s₂ for 2 steps.","Step i: Run M on s₁, s₂, …, sᵢ for i steps.","Whenever M accepts a string sₚ, print sₚ."]},{"page":13,"title":"Variant 3: Enumerators","details":["Equivalence Logic (Directional Breakdown)","⇐ Direction (If an Enumerator exists, a Recognizer TM M exists):","Given input w, M runs the Enumerator E. As E prints strings s₁, s₂, …, M compares each printed string to w. If w = sᵢ, M halts and ACCEPTS."]},{"page":14,"title":"TM Variant Architectural Equivalent Model Simulation Technique Complexity Impact","details":["Feature Multitape TM k Tapes, k heads Standard 1-Tape Multi-symbol tape with Quadratic time TM delimiters (#) and dotted slowdown (O(n²))","head markers (ȧ)","Nondeterministic TM Multi-branch choices Standard 1-Tape 3-Tape BFS exploration Exponential time per state TM of the computation tree slowdown (O(2ⁿ))","Enumerator Tape control + Recognizer TM Dovetailing simulation N/A (Functional Printer output over input strings Σ* equivalence)"]},{"page":15,"title":"Encoding a Machine as Data (⟨M⟩)","details":["The Core Premise: Every component of a Turing Machine M = (Q, Σ, Γ, δ, q₀, qaccept, qreject) is finite. Therefore, any TM can be serialized into a plain binary text string.","A Simple Binary Encoding Scheme:","States: q₁, q₂, q₃, … ⇒ 1, 11, 111, … Tape Symbols: 0, 1, ⊔ ⇒ 1, 11, 111","Directions: L, R ⇒ 1, 11"]},{"page":16,"title":"Encoding a Machine as Data (⟨M⟩)","details":["Encoding a Transition Rule: The rule δ(q₁, 0) = (q₂, 1, R) becomes a string separated by zeros:","Code = 1 0 1 0 11 0 11 0 11 Encoding the Full Machine ⟨M⟩: Chain all transition codes together separated by double zeros (00).","Key Conclusion:","Any Turing Machine M ⟺ A finite binary string ⟨M⟩ ∈ {0, 1}*"]},{"page":17,"title":"The Universal Turing Machine (UTM)","details":["A Universal Turing Machine (U) is a single, fixed Turing Machine that takes two inputs on its tape: a program description ⟨M⟩ and an input string w.","U(⟨M, w⟩) = Simulates M on w How U Operates (3-Tape View):","Tape 1: Stores the code of M (⟨M⟩).","Tape 2: Stores M's virtual tape (initialized with w).","Tape 3: Stores M's current state."]},{"page":18,"title":"The Universal Turing Machine (UTM)","details":["Tape 1 (Code): # 1 0 1 0 1 1 0 1 1 0 1 1 # ...","Tape 2 (Data): # a b b a # ...","Tape 3 (State): # q3 # The Simulation Cycle:","1. Look at M's current state on Tape 3 and the current symbol on Tape 2.","2. Scan Tape 1 to find the matching transition rule in ⟨M⟩.","3. Update Tape 2 (write symbol, move head) and Tape 3 (update state).","4. If M enters qaccept, U accepts. If M enters qreject, U rejects."]},{"page":19,"title":"The Birth of Software","details":["Before Alan Turing (1936): Calculators were single-purpose hardware. If you wanted a machine to calculate trajectories, you built Machine A. If you wanted to compute mortgage payments, you physically built Machine B.","Turing's Insight (The UTM): You don't need different hardware machines for different tasks. You build ONE single hardware machine (U) that reads a description of another machine (⟨M⟩) from memory and simulates it.","Universal Turing Machine (U) ⇔ Operating System / CPU Machine Encoding (⟨M⟩) ⇔ Software / Python Script / .exe file Input w ⇔ Data File / User Input"]},{"page":20,"title":"Questions?","details":["thisaraw@boffin.lk"]}],"overview":"Turing machine variants alter how calculations are organized without changing which languages are computable. Multiple tapes, nondeterminism, and other conveniences can substantially improve execution time or clarity of construction.","takeaway":"Equivalent computability does not imply equivalent running time.","points":[{"title":"Multitape transition","body":"For k tapes, δ: Q × Γᵏ → Q × Γᵏ × {L,R,S}ᵏ, reading and writing k symbols and specifying k head movements."},{"title":"Simulating many tapes with one","body":"A single tape encodes multiple virtual tapes separated by markers and records each virtual head location by marking corresponding symbols."},{"title":"Computational equivalence","body":"Every multitape TM can be simulated by a single-tape TM. Nondeterministic and deterministic TMs also recognize the same Turing-recognizable languages."},{"title":"Runtime tradeoff","body":"A simple one-tape simulation of a multitape machine may use quadratic time overhead, even though the recognized language stays unchanged."},{"title":"Three-block equality","body":"For aⁿbⁿcⁿ, a machine can mark or count corresponding symbols across all three blocks and verify that the input is exhausted together."}]},{"id":10,"title":"Decidability","subtitle":"Deciders, recognizers, and undecidability","group":"Computability","slides":31,"sections":[{"page":2,"title":"The Search for a Definition of \"Algorithm\"","details":["Hilbert's Challenge (1900): At the International Congress of Mathematicians, David Hilbert presented 23 fundamental problems for the 20th century.","Problem #10 (Polynomial Diophantine Equations): Devise a \"general mechanical procedure\" to determine whether an arbitrary polynomial equation with integer coefficients has integer roots.","Example: 3x² − 2xy − z⁷ = 0 ⟸?⟹ x, y, z ∈ ℤ The Mathematical Obstacle:","To show a problem can be solved, you only need to invent one specific working algorithm.","To show a problem cannot be solved, you must prove that no possible algorithm could ever exist.","The Core Gap: In 1900, mathematics lacked a precise, formal definition of what an \"algorithm\" actually was."]},{"page":3,"title":"The Church-Turing Thesis","details":["The Breakthrough (1936): Two independent mathematical frameworks were published to formally define computable functions:","Alonzo Church (λ-Calculus): Formalized computation using functional abstraction, variable binding, and substitution rules (λ𝑥.M)N.","Alan Turing (Turing Machines): Formalized computation using a mechanical model mimicking a human mathematician reading/writing on paper tape.","The Equivalence Theorem: Church and Turing proved that λ-Calculus and Turing Machines are computationally equivalent. Any function computable by one model is computable by the other.","Core Definition: The intuitive, informal concept of an algorithm (or effective procedure) is identical to the formal mathematical concept of a Turing Machine that halts on all inputs (a Decider).","Intuitive Concept of an Algorithm ⟺ Turing Machine Decider"]},{"page":4,"title":"Robustness and the Universality of Computation","details":["Hardware Independence: Adding fancy features (multiple tapes, random access memory pointers, non-determinism, quantum superpositions) changes execution speed, but never changes the set of solvable problems.","The Universal Boundary:","Python / C++ / Java Code ≡ Supercomputers ≡ 1-Tape Turing Machine The Direct Consequence for Decidability:","If a problem can be solved by a Turing Machine ⇒ It is algorithmic / decidable.","If a problem cannot be solved by a Turing Machine ⇒ It is fundamentally impossible for ANY algorithm or physical computer to solve."]},{"page":5,"title":"The Halting Problem (HALTTM)","details":["The Problem Statement: Can we build a universal program analyzer that inspects any given computer program and its input, and determines whether that program will eventually finish running or get stuck in an infinite loop?","Formal Language Definition:","HALTTM = { ⟨M, w⟩ | M is a Turing Machine and M halts on input w }","The Target Decider (H): We want a decider machine H that takes an encoding ⟨M, w⟩ and behaves as follows:","H(⟨M, w⟩) = ACCEPT if M halts on input w H(⟨M, w⟩) = REJECT if M loops forever on input w Core Goal: Prove whether such a decider H can exist as a valid algorithm."]},{"page":6,"title":"The Halting Problem: The Proof","details":["Step 1 (Hypothesis): Assume that HALTTM is decidable. Therefore, a Turing Machine decider H exists that always halts and correctly identifies whether M(w) halts or loops.","Step 2 (Constructing Machine D): Use H as a subroutine to build a new, malicious Turing Machine D.","Input to D: A description of a Turing Machine ⟨M⟩.","Internal Behavior of D:","D runs H on input ⟨M, ⟨M⟩⟩ (testing what machine M does when fed its own source code as input).","D does the exact opposite of whatever H outputs:","If H outputs ACCEPT (M halts on ⟨M⟩) ⇒ D intentionally enters an infinite loop.","If H outputs REJECT (M loops on ⟨M⟩) ⇒ D halts immediately and accepts."]},{"page":7,"title":"The Halting Problem: The Proof","details":["Step 3: Now run Machine D with its own source code ⟨D⟩ as input: D(⟨ D⟩ )","Case 1: Suppose D(⟨D⟩) HALTS.","H(⟨D, ⟨D⟩⟩) must output ACCEPT (because H is a decider that predicts D halts).","By D's internal construction, when H outputs ACCEPT, D enters an infinite loop.","Result: D(⟨D⟩) LOOPS FOREVER ⇒ Contradiction!","Case 2: Suppose D(⟨D⟩) LOOPS FOREVER.","H(⟨D, ⟨D⟩⟩) must output REJECT (because H predicts D loops).","By D's internal construction, when H outputs REJECT, D halts immediately.","Result: D(⟨D⟩) HALTS ⇒ Contradiction!","Summary: D(⟨D⟩) Halts ⟺ D(⟨D⟩) Loops Forever"]},{"page":8,"title":"Turing’s Undecidability Theorem","details":["Theorem (Alan Turing, 1936): HALTTM is undecidable. No general algorithm exists to determine whether an arbitrary program halts on a given input.","Conclusion of the Proof: The logical paradox D(⟨D⟩) ⟺ ¬D(⟨D⟩) proves that our initial assumption was false. The decider machine H cannot exist.","Takeaway for Computer Science:","No Perfect Static Analyzers: It is mathematically impossible to write a program that checks all other programs for infinite loops, deadlocks, or runtime exceptions.","The Ultimate Ceiling: Unlike the Pumping Lemma for Finite Automata (which showed anbn just needed a stack), hitting HALTTM proves that no computing device, past, present, or future, can overcome this boundary."]},{"page":9,"title":"Decidable (R) vs. Recognizable (RE) Languages","details":["Having established that HALTTM hits a computational wall, we formally categorize languages based on how Turing Machines handle them:","Input String w Turing Machine qaccept qreject Infinite Loop (Halts & Accepts) (Halts & Rejects) (Never Halts)"]},{"page":10,"title":"Decidable (R) vs. Recognizable (RE) Languages","details":["1. Turing-Decidable Languages (R) 2. Turing-Recognizable Languages (RE)","\"Recursive\" \"Recursively Enumerable\"","Definition: Definition:","A language L is decidable if a Turing Machine A language L is recognizable if a Turing M exists such that: Machine M exists such that:","w ≡ w ∈ L ⇒ M halts in qaccept w ∈ L ⇒ M halts in qaccept w ≡ w ∉ L ⇒ M halts in qreject w ∉ L ⇒ M halts in qreject OR loops forever (∞)","Key Feature:","M is a Decider - infinite loops are strictly Key Feature:","prohibited for all inputs. Acceptance is guaranteed in finite time, but rejection may hang indefinitely."]},{"page":11,"title":"Proving R ⫋ RE (Decidable is a Strict Subset)","details":["Is HALTTM Turing-Recognizable? YES.","Recognizer Construction (S): Given input ⟨M, w⟩, simulator S simply runs M on w.","If M halts ⇒ S accepts. If M loops ⇒ S loops.","Thus, HALTTM ∈ RE.","Is HALTTM Turing-Decidable? NO.","Turing’s proof demonstrated that no decider can avoid infinite loops for all pairs ⟨M, w⟩.","Thus, HALTTM ∉ R.","Conclusion: HALTTM ∈ RE \\ R ⇒ R ⫋ RE"]},{"page":12,"title":"The Complement Theorem","details":["Theorem: A language L is decidable if and only if L is Turing-recognizable (L ∈ RE) AND its complement L̅ is Turing-recognizable (L̅ ∈ RE).","Decidable (R) = RE ∩ co-RE Proof Intuition:","Run the recognizer for L (M₁) and the recognizer for L̅ (M₂) in parallel on input w. Since w must belong to either L or L̅, exactly one machine is guaranteed to accept and halt. Thus, the parallel combination never loops forever."]},{"page":13,"title":"Beyond Recognizability (RE̅)","details":["The Complement Theorem","Theorem: A language L is decidable if and only if L is Turing-recognizable (L ∈ RE) AND its complement L̅ is Turing-recognizable (L̅ ∈ RE).","Decidable (R) = RE ∩ co-RE Proof Intuition: Run recognizer M₁ for L and recognizer M₂ for L̅ in parallel on input w. Since w belongs to either L or L̅, exactly one machine accepts and halts. Thus, the combination never loops forever.","The Unrecognizable Boundary (HALT̅TM)","We know HALTTM ∈ RE (it is Turing-recognizable).","We know HALTTM ∉ R (it is undecidable).","By the Complement Theorem, the complement HALT̅ₘₘ (machines that loop forever) cannot be Turing-recognizable:","HALT̅TM ∉ RE"]},{"page":14,"title":"The Master Hierarchy of Computability","details":["ALL LANGUAGES / PROBLEMS Uncountable Set: 2ℵ₀ TURING-RECOGNIZABLE / RE Countable Set: ℵ₀ UNRECOGNIZABLE / NON-RE (Outside RE Boundary)","RE \\ R Examples: HALTTM , ATM HALTε","Examples:","TURING-DECIDABLE / R Countable Set: ℵ₀ Complement HALTTM ĀTM Decidable Problem Examples:","ADFA EDFA EQDFA ACFG ECFG"]},{"page":15,"title":"Countable vs. Uncountable Sets","details":["Definition of Countable: A set S is countably infinite if its elements can be put into a one-to-one correspondence (1-to-1) with the set of natural numbers ℕ = {1, 2, 3, …}.","An explicit listing exists: S = {s₁, s₂, s₃, s₄, …}","Fact 1: The Set of All Strings (Σ*) is Countable We can list all strings in lexicographic order:","Σ* = {ε, 0, 1, 00, 01, 10, 11, 000, …}","Fact 2: The Set of All Turing Machines is Countable Every Turing Machine M can be encoded as a finite string of bytes ⟨M⟩. Sorting these byte strings lexicographically gives a complete, numbered list of all possible programs:","All TMs = {M₁, M₂, M₃, M₄, …}"]},{"page":16,"title":"Cantor’s Diagonalization Method (1891)","details":["Goal: Prove that the set of all possible languages (power set 𝒫(Σ*)) cannot be listed in a countable sequence.","The Infinite Binary Matrix: Construct a grid where row i represents Turing Machine Mᵢ, column j represents string sj, and cell (i, j) is 1 if Mᵢ accepts sj, and 0 if it rejects/loops:","s₁ s₂ s₃ s₄ … M₁ 1 0 1 0 … M₂ 1 1 0 0 … M₃ 0 1 0 1 … M₄ 1 0 1 1 …"]},{"page":17,"title":"Uncomputable Languages Outnumber Computable Ones","details":["Constructing the Diagonal Language (Ld): Define a new language Ld by flipping every bit along the main diagonal:","si ∈ Ld ⟺ Mi does NOT accept si Ld's row pattern: 0, 0, 1, 0, … The Contradiction: Is there any Turing Machine Mk in our complete list that recognizes Ld?","Test Mk on string sk:","If Mk accepts sk, then by definition of Ld, sk ∉ Ld.","If Mk does not accept sk, then by definition of Ld, sk ∈ Ld.","Mk differs from Ld at string sk for every single k."]},{"page":18,"title":"Uncomputable Languages Outnumber Computable Ones","details":["Mathematical Conclusion:","|Turing Machines| = ℵ0 (Countably Infinite)","|All Languages| = 2ℵ0 (Uncountably Infinite)","Turing Machines (ℵ0) ≪ Languages (2ℵ0)"]},{"page":19,"title":"Reducibility & The Limits of Computation","details":["Reducibility is a way of converting one problem into another problem.","If problem A reduces to problem B, it means: \"If I have a magical tool to solve B, I can use it to solve A.\"","Example:","Problem A: Finding your way around a new city.","Problem B: Reading a map.","Reduction: If you can solve B (read a map), you automatically solve A. Therefore, A is no harder than B.","Using Reductions to Prove Impossibility:","If A reduces to B, and we already know A is impossible to solve, what does that say about B?","Conclusion: B must also be impossible to solve","Logic: If B were solvable, we could use it to solve A. But mathematics has proven A is unsolvable. Therefore, the solver for B cannot exist."]},{"page":20,"title":"The Direction of Reduction","details":["Notation: A ≤ₖ B (Problem A mapping-reduces to Problem B)","To prove a New Problem (B) is undecidable, we must reduce from a Known Undecidable Problem (A) to the New Problem (B).","The Correct Formula:","Known Undecidable ≤ₘ New Unknown Problem","Assume: Start by assuming the target problem B is decidable by some Turing Machine R.","Construct: Build a wrapper Turing Machine S to solve the known undecidable problem A (e.g., the Halting Problem).","Wrap: Inside S, format the input and feed it into the hypothetical decider R.","Contradict: Show that S works perfectly. Because A is proven undecidable, S cannot exist. Thus, our assumption was false: R cannot exist, and B is undecidable."]},{"page":22,"title":"Linking to the Halting Problem","details":["Our Anchor for Reductions The Halting Problem (HALTₖₖ):","HALTₘₘ = { ⟨M, w⟩ │ TM M halts on input w }","We know HALTₖₖ is undecidable (Turing, 1936). We will now use HALTₖₖ as our \"Problem A\" to prove three highly practical computer science problems are also undecidable."]},{"page":23,"title":"Exercise 1 - The Blank Tape Problem","details":["Can we write a static analysis tool that tells us if a program will eventually halt if we give it absolutely no input (a completely blank tape)?","HALTε = { ⟨M⟩ │ M halts when run on a blank tape }","Exercise: Using reducibility (HALTTM ≤m HALTε), prove that HALTε is undecidable."]},{"page":24,"title":"Exercise 1 - Solution","details":["Proof by Reduction:","1. Assume a decider R exists that solves HALTε.","2. Construct a machine S to solve HALTₖₖ on input ⟨M, w⟩:","Inside S, create the code for a new machine Mw:","- Machine Mw: Ignore the input tape. Overwrite it with string w. Run M on w.","Pass ⟨Mw⟩ into our hypothetical decider R.","If R accepts, S accepts. If R rejects, S rejects.","3. Contradiction: Mw halts on a blank tape if and only if M halts on w. We just used R to solve the general Halting Problem!","4. Since HALTₖₖ is undecidable, R cannot exist. Therefore, HALTε is undecidable."]},{"page":25,"title":"Exercise 2 - Dead Code Detection","details":["In compiler optimization, we want to know if a specific line of code (state q) is \"dead code\" (never reached).","STATEₘₘ = { ⟨M, w, q⟩ │ M enters state q when run on w }","Exercise: Prove that STATEₖₖ is undecidable by reducing HALTₖₖ to it."]},{"page":26,"title":"Exercise 2 - Solution","details":["Proof by Reduction:","1. Assume a decider R exists for STATEₖₖ.","2. Construct a machine S to solve HALTₖₖ on input ⟨M, w⟩:","Inside S, modify M to create M':","- Add a brand new state called qtrap.","- Redirect all halting transitions (accept, reject, and blank cells) to point to qtrap.","Run the decider R on ⟨M', w, qtrap⟩.","If R says YES, S accepts. If R says NO, S rejects.","3. Contradiction: M' reaches qₖᵣₐₖ if and only if original M halts. S just solved the Halting Problem.","4. Therefore, STATEₖₖ is undecidable. (Perfect dead-code elimination is impossible!)"]},{"page":27,"title":"Exercise 3 - The Emptiness Problem","details":["Can we write an algorithm to determine if a Turing Machine rejects everything? (i.e., its language is completely empty).","Eₘₘ = { ⟨M⟩ │ L(M) = ∅ }","Exercise: Prove Eₖₖ is undecidable by reducing the Acceptance Problem (Aₖₖ) to Eₖₖ."]},{"page":28,"title":"Exercise 3 - Solution","details":["Proof by Reduction:","1. Assume a decider R exists for Eₖₖ.","2. Construct a machine S to solve Aₖₖ on input ⟨M, w⟩:","Inside S, build a new machine M₁:","- Machine M₁ (on input x): Ignore x. Simulate original M on string w. If M accepts w, Accept x.","Run R on ⟨M₁⟩.","Notice the flip: If R accepts (says L(M₁) is empty), M did not accept w, so S should Reject. If R rejects, S should Accept.","3. Contradiction: S successfully decides Aₖₖ. But Aₖₖ is undecidable.","4. Therefore, Eₖₖ is undecidable."]},{"page":29,"title":"Rice's Theorem","details":["Any non-trivial semantic property of the language recognized by a Turing machine is undecidable.","The Two Criteria:","Semantic Property: It is about the language L(M) (what the code accepts), not the syntax/code of M (e.g., how many states it has, or if it executes line 4).","Non-trivial: At least one TM's language has the property, and at least one TM's language does not.","Why it matters for students:","\"Does M accept the string 'hello'?\" ⇒ Undecidable by Rice's Theorem.","\"Does M accept a regular language?\" ⇒ Undecidable by Rice's Theorem.","\"Does M accept an empty language (Eₖₖ)?\" ⇒ Undecidable by Rice's Theorem."]},{"page":30,"title":"Formal Mapping Reducibility (A ≤ₘ B) & Recognizability","details":["The Formal Definition: A ≤ₖ B if there exists a computable function f: Σ* → Σ* such that for every string w:","w ∈ A ⇔ f(w) ∈ B The Four Decision & Recognition Rules (Standard Multiple-Choice / True-False Material):","If A ≤ₖ B and B is decidable, then A is decidable.","If A ≤ₖ B and A is undecidable, then B is undecidable.","If A ≤ₖ B and B is Turing-recognizable, then A is Turing-recognizable.","If A ≤ₖ B and A is NOT Turing-recognizable, then B is NOT Turing-recognizable."]},{"page":31,"title":"Questions?","details":["thisaraw@boffin.lk"]}],"overview":"Decidability formalizes the difference between problems having algorithms that halt on every input and problems for which no such general algorithm exists. The halting problem and diagonalization show a hard boundary to automatic program analysis.","takeaway":"Recognizable does not mean decidable: a machine that can eventually accept a yes-instance might never halt on a no-instance.","points":[{"title":"Church–Turing thesis","body":"The informal notion of effectively computable procedures is modeled by Turing-computable functions. The equivalence of various formal models is mathematical; the thesis connecting them to an informal notion is not itself a theorem."},{"title":"Decidable languages","body":"A language is decidable if some TM halts and correctly accepts or rejects every possible input."},{"title":"Turing-recognizable languages","body":"A recognizer halts and accepts yes-instances but may either reject or run forever on no-instances."},{"title":"Halting problem","body":"HALT_TM asks whether machine M halts on input w. No TM decides HALT_TM for every encoded (M,w) pair."},{"title":"Diagonalization idea","body":"Assuming a universal halting decider leads to a specially constructed program that behaves contrary to that decider’s prediction when fed its own encoding."},{"title":"Complement criterion","body":"A language is decidable exactly when both the language and its complement are Turing-recognizable."},{"title":"Reductions","body":"To prove a new problem undecidable, transform instances of a known undecidable problem into instances of the target. The reduction direction is essential."}]},{"id":11,"title":"Computational complexity","subtitle":"P, NP, and tractability","group":"Complexity","slides":11,"sections":[{"page":2,"title":"The Need for Efficiency","details":["Decidability asks: \"Can this problem be solved by an algorithm at all?\" Complexity theory asks: \"Can this problem be solved using realistic amounts of time and memory?\" A problem may be completely decidable, yet require 2¹⁰⁰⁰ steps to solve on an input of length 100.","An algorithm whose runtime exceeds the lifespan of the universe is computationally useless.","Complexity shifts our focus from computability (solvable vs. unsolvable) to tractability (feasible vs. infeasible)."]},{"page":3,"title":"Measuring Time on Turing Machines","details":["Let M be a deterministic Turing Machine that halts on all inputs.","The running time or time complexity of M is the function f: ℕ → ℕ, where f(n) is the maximum number of steps that M uses on any input of length n (worst-case analysis).","Definition of Time Complexity Classes:","TIME(t(n)) = { L | L is a language decided by an O(t(n))-time deterministic TM }","This formalizes the standard Big-O runtime classifications (O(n), O(n log n), O(n²), O(2ⁿ))","on a rigorous mathematical model."]},{"page":4,"title":"The Class P (Polynomial Time)","details":["Definition: P = ⋃k≥1 TIME(nᵏ)","P is the class of languages that are decidable in polynomial time by a Deterministic single-tape Turing Machine.","The Cobham-Edmonds Thesis: Polynomial time (O(nᵏ)) mathematically formalizes the boundary of \"tractable\" or practically feasible computation.","Problems decidable in polynomial time scale with improvements in computing hardware;","exponential problems do not."]},{"page":5,"title":"Classic DSA Problems in P","details":["Shortest Path: Given a directed graph, edge weights, and two nodes s and t, find the shortest path. Solved in polynomial time by Dijkstra's algorithm (O(|E| + |V| log |V|)) or BFS (O(|V| + |E|)).","Minimum Spanning Tree: Solved by Prim's or Kruskal's algorithm in O(|E| log |V|).","Sorting & Searching: MergeSort runs in O(n log n) steps; Binary Search runs in O(log n).","Graph Connectivity: Determining whether a path exists between s and t (PATH problem) is decidable in O(n²) time."]},{"page":6,"title":"The Class NP: Definition 1 (Nondeterministic TM)","details":["Critical Rule: NP does NOT stand for \"Non-Polynomial.\" NP stands for Nondeterministic Polynomial Time.","Definition: NP = ⋃ k≥1 NTIME(nᵏ)","NP is the class of languages decidable in polynomial time by a Nondeterministic Turing Machine (NTM).","Recall NTM Execution: An NTM branches at every step. It accepts an input if at least one computation path reaches qaccept within nᵏ steps."]},{"page":7,"title":"The Class NP: Definition 2 (Polynomial Verifier)","details":["An equivalent, more intuitive definition of NP: A language L is in NP if and only if it has a polynomial-time deterministic verifier.","A verifier V is an algorithm that takes two inputs:","The input string w (the problem instance).","A certificate or witness c (the proposed solution/proof).","Formal Criterion:","w ∈ L ⇔ there exists a polynomial-length certificate c such that V(⟨w, c⟩) accepts in polynomial time","Summary: NP is the set of all decision problems whose solutions can be verified in polynomial time."]},{"page":8,"title":"The P versus NP Question","details":["Because every deterministic TM is trivially a nondeterministic TM with only one branch:","P ⊆ NP The Million-Dollar Question (Clay Millennium Prize): Does P = NP?","Informally: If a problem's solution can be verified efficiently, can it always be found efficiently?","Consensus: Most theoretical computer scientists believe P ≠ NP, but no formal proof currently exists.","If P = NP, then every puzzle that is easy to check is equally easy to solve, breaking modern public-key cryptography."]},{"page":9,"title":"Polynomial-Time Reductions (A ≤ₚ B)","details":["Connecting Reducibility to Complexity: In computability theory, we used mapping reductions (A ≤ₚ B) to transfer undecidability. In complexity theory, we add an execution budget: the reduction function must compute its mapping in polynomial time.","Definition: Language A is polynomial-time mapping reducible to language B (A ≤ₚ B) if a polynomial-time computable function f: Σ* → Σ* exists such that:","w ∈ A ⇔ f(w) ∈ B Key Theorem: If A ≤ₚ B and B ∈ P, then A ∈ P."]},{"page":10,"title":"NP-Hard and NP-Complete","details":["NP-Hard: A language H is NP-hard if every problem in NP reduces to it in polynomial time:","∀ A ∈ NP, A ≤ₚ H (NP-hard problems are at least as hard as the hardest problems in NP; they do not have to be inside NP themselves).","NP-Complete (NPC): A language B is NP-Complete if:","1. B ∈ NP (solutions can be verified in polynomial time), AND","2. B is NP-hard (every problem in NP reduces to B in polynomial time).","Significance: The NP-complete problems are the hardest problems in NP. If any single NP-complete problem can be solved in polynomial time, then P = NP."]},{"page":11,"title":"Questions?","details":["thisaraw@boffin.lk"]}],"overview":"Computational complexity examines how resource consumption grows with input size for problems that are computable. Complexity classes such as P and NP describe distinct guarantees about deterministic solving and efficiently verifiable certificates.","takeaway":"P versus NP remains open: nobody has proved whether every efficiently verifiable problem is efficiently solvable.","points":[{"title":"Worst-case time","body":"For a decider M, f(n) is the maximum number of steps M takes over all inputs of length n."},{"title":"Class P","body":"P consists of decision problems solvable in polynomial time by a deterministic TM: P = ⋃ₖ≥₁ TIME(nᵏ)."},{"title":"Class NP","body":"NP consists of decision problems with polynomial-time verifiable certificates (equivalently solvable in polynomial time on a nondeterministic TM)."},{"title":"Relationships","body":"P ⊆ NP is known. Whether P = NP is an open problem."},{"title":"NP-hard versus NP-complete","body":"A problem is NP-hard if every problem in NP reduces to it in polynomial time. It is NP-complete when it is both NP-hard and itself in NP."},{"title":"Polynomial-time reductions","body":"A reduction A ≤ₚ B shows that an efficient algorithm for B would yield one for A. To establish a target as NP-hard, reduce a known NP-hard problem to the target."},{"title":"Theory versus practice","body":"Polynomial time is a formal tractability proxy, not a promise of useful speed; exponentials can work on small instances and high-degree polynomials can be impractical."}]}];